/* Gnamma Stage 84: provisional page-aligned source records; not a certified critical edition. */
window.__GNAMMA_SANANDAJI84.push(...[{"id":"I194.upper.01","volume":1,"page":194,"physical_pdf_page":207,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"مع بقائه بشخصه ويسمى بعدا مفطورا لأنه فطر عليه البديهة فإننا نشاهد بأن الماء إنما يحصل فيما بين أطراف الإناء من الفضاء قيل كأنه جوهر متوسط بين عالم الجواهر المجردة التي لا تقبل إشارة حسية والأجسام الكثيفة التي تقبلها","english":"—while remaining numerically the same. It is called a naturally apprehended extension, because immediate apprehension is naturally disposed to it: we observe water occupying the space between the sides of a vessel. It has been said to be, as it were, a substance intermediate between the world of separate substances, which cannot be sensibly indicated, and dense bodies, which can.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I193.upper.03","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Native-source enlargement confirms البديهة; مفطور is clearly printed. This is proof against the base scan, not an independent-witness collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I194.upper.02","volume":1,"page":194,"physical_pdf_page":207,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والأمارات) التي ذكروها للمكان (مثل مساواة المكان للمتمكن) لأن المتمكن يجب أن يكون منطبقا على المكان المساواة فيجب أن يكونا متساويين (و) مثل (عمومه) أي المكان (لكل جسم) لأن كل جسم مشار إليه بأنه هنا أو هناك والمشار إليه بهذه هو المكان ليس إلا","english":"“The indications” they give for place include “place’s equality with the occupying body”: the occupant must coincide exactly with place, so the two must be equal. “And” they include “its being common to every body,” since every body is indicated as being here or there, and what “here” and “there” indicate is place itself.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I194.upper.03","volume":1,"page":194,"physical_pdf_page":207,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) مثل (كون الطير في الهواء المتحرك عليه ساكنا والحجر في الماء الجاري عليه ساكنا تدل) تلك الأمارات الثلاث (على الثاني) أي كون المكان بعدا فإنه لو كان سطحا لزم عدم مساواته له فيما إذا جعلنا الشمعة المدورة صفحة رقيقة فإن السطح المحيط بها","english":"“And” they include “a bird’s remaining stationary while air moves around it, and a stone’s remaining stationary while water flows around it.” These three indications “support the second” view, that place is extension. If place were a surface, it would fail to remain equal to the occupant when a round piece of wax is made into a thin sheet: the surrounding surface—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I195.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I195.lower.01","volume":1,"page":195,"physical_pdf_page":208,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"من أن هذا الاستدلال بظاهره إنما يدل على أن كل جسم هو نفس المكان وهو غير المطلوب","english":"—that this argument, on its surface, proves only that every body is place itself, which is not what is sought.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I194.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I195.lower.02","volume":1,"page":195,"physical_pdf_page":208,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مع أن المتمكن في الحالتين واحد الخ) أقول إن أريد بوحدة المتمكن هنا وحدة تمام مقدار الشمعة في الحالتين فهو مسلم لكن لا يلزم منه عدم مساواة المكان للمتمكن فإن المساواة المسلمة عند القائلين بالسطح هو تساوي مقدار المكان لمقدار السطح الظاهر من المتمكن لا لتمام مقداره كما سبق فهنا يساوي مقدار كل من المكانين لما يحويه من مقدار السطح الظاهر من الشمعة والتفاوت بين السطحين الظاهرين من الشمعة في الحالتين المقتضي للتفاوت بين المكانين لا ينافي وحدة الشمعة بالنظر إلى تمام مقدارها كما هو ظاهر","english":"On “Although the occupant is the same in both states, etc.” I say: if sameness here means the sameness of the wax’s total magnitude in both states, that is conceded. But it does not entail inequality between place and occupant. The equality accepted by proponents of the surface theory is equality between place’s magnitude and the magnitude of the occupant’s outer surface, not its total magnitude, as explained above. Each of the two places is therefore equal in magnitude to the outer surface of the wax that it contains. The difference between the wax’s outer surfaces in the two states, which entails a difference between the two places, does not conflict with the wax’s sameness in total magnitude. This is evident.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I195.lower.03","volume":1,"page":195,"physical_pdf_page":208,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وإن أريد بوحدة المتمكن وحدة السطح الظاهر من الشمعة في الحالتين فهو مع كونه ممنوعا كما يأتي التصريح بها في الجواب يناقض تسليم كون السطح المحيط بها حالة الرقة أضعاف المحيط بها حالة التدوير فتدبر فإنه دقيق جدا","english":"If sameness of the occupant means sameness of the wax’s outer surface in the two states, then, besides being disputed—as the reply explicitly states—it contradicts the concession that the surrounding surface when the wax is thin is many times the surrounding surface when it is round. Reflect, for this is very subtle.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I195.lower.04","volume":1,"page":195,"physical_pdf_page":208,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولزم أيضا أن يحوي الجسم المحيط الخ) أي واللازم باطل ضرورة تناهي الأبعاد (قوله ضرورة تبدل السطوح الخ) أي والحركة إما عبارة عن ذلك التبدل أو ملزومة له","english":"On “It would also follow that the encompassing body is enclosed, etc.” That consequence is false because dimensions must be finite. On “Since the surfaces necessarily change, etc.” The argument takes motion either to consist in that change or necessarily to accompany it.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I195.lower.05","volume":1,"page":195,"physical_pdf_page":208,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهذه الحالة غير استبدال المكان الخ) أي أن هذه الحالة التي هي الحركة غير استبدال السطوح ولو قيل يلزمه لما قلنا فلا نسلم أنه يلزم من استبدال السطوح المحيطة بالطير مثلا حصول تلك الحالة لجواز أن يكون لازما أعم ثم اعلم أن القائلين بالسطح احتجوا بأن المكان لو كان هو البعد","english":"On “That condition is not identical with exchanging places, etc.” This condition, motion, is not the exchange of surfaces. Even if it is said to accompany motion necessarily, we still do not concede that an exchange of surfaces surrounding a bird, for example, entails the condition in question: it may be a more general concomitant. Know, moreover, that proponents of the surface theory argue that, if place were extension—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I196.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I195.upper.01","volume":1,"page":195,"physical_pdf_page":208,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"أضعاف المحيط بالمدورة مع أن المتمكن في الحالتين واحد ولزم أيضا أن يحوي الجسم المحيط بالكل جسم آخر يكون سطحه الباطن مكانا له وإلا لم يكن المكان عاما لكل جسم وأن يكون الطير في الهواء المتحرك متحركا وكذا الحجر في الماء الجاري ضرورة تبدل السطوح المحيطة بهما","english":"—would be many times that surrounding the round piece, although the occupant is the same in both states. It would also follow that the body encompassing the whole must itself be enclosed by another body whose inner surface is its place; otherwise place would not belong to every body. Further, a bird amid moving air, and likewise a stone in flowing water, would be moving, since the surfaces surrounding them necessarily change.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I194.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I195.upper.02","volume":1,"page":195,"physical_pdf_page":208,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"والجواب بمنع كون المتمكن في الحالتين واحدا ومنع عموم المكان لأن المشار إليه بهذه قد يكون تمامه وإلا فالجسم في الإشارة الحسية أي الوضع الذي به يمتاز المشار إليه عن غيره وأن الحركة عندهم حالة مستمرة للمتحرك من أول المسافة إلى آخرها وهذه الحالة غير استبدال المكان هذا","english":"The reply denies that the occupant is the same in the two states and denies that place belongs to every body. What is indicated by these expressions may be [the body] in its entirety; otherwise the body possesses a sensible position—that is, the disposition by which the indicated thing is distinguished from others. Moreover, for them motion is a continuous condition of the moving thing from the beginning of a distance to its end, and that condition is not identical with exchanging places. Such is the account.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"قد يكون تمامه وإلا فالجسم في الإشارة الحسية","reading":"قد يكون تمامه وإلا فالجسم في الإشارة الحسية","status":"This short response to the universality argument is textually difficult; the English is provisional and must not be treated as a settled reconstruction."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I196.lower.01","volume":1,"page":196,"physical_pdf_page":209,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"فأما أن يكون متوهما مفروضا أو متحققا موجودا والكل باطل أما الأول فلأن المكان ضرورة وأما الثاني فلأنه حينئذ إن كان قابلا للحركة الأينية كان له مكان وينتقل إليه فيلزم ترتب الأمكنة لا إلى نهاية وإن لم يكن قابلا لها لزم أن لا يكون الجسم أيضا قابلا للحركة فإن الجسم ملازم البعد المنافي لقبولها وملازم المنافي للشيء مناف لذلك الشيء","english":"—it would be either imagined and posited or actually existent; both alternatives are false. The first is rejected because place [exists] necessarily. As for the second: if this extension admits local movement, it must have a place to which it moves, producing an endless series of places. If it does not admit local movement, the body likewise cannot admit movement, because the body is inseparable from an extension incompatible with movement, and what is inseparable from something incompatible with a thing is itself incompatible with that thing.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I195.lower.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I196.lower.02","volume":1,"page":196,"physical_pdf_page":209,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ولأنه يلزم تمكن الجسم في البعدين تداخل البعد وهو باطل للقطع بأنه ليس في الإناء المملوء من الماء إلا بعد واحد ولأنه يستلزم اجتماع المثلين في محل هو المتمكن وبأن البعد في نفسه إما أن يفتقر إلى محل فيمتنع تجرده أو يستغني منه فلا يحل في المادة لأن معنى الحلول اختصاصه به بحيث لا يتقوم بدونه فلا يرد أنه يجوز أن لا يفتقر إلى المحل بالذات لكن بعرضه الحلول فيه بالغرض","english":"Furthermore, a body’s occupancy of two extensions entails the interpenetration of extensions, which is false: we are certain that a vessel full of water contains only one extension. It also entails two things of the same kind coinciding in one subject, namely the occupying body. They further argue that extension in itself either requires a subject, in which case it cannot be separate, or does not, in which case it cannot inhere in matter. Inherence means its being attached specifically to that subject so that it cannot subsist without it. It is therefore no reply to say that it may not require a subject in itself but may accidentally come to inhere in one.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"لكن بعرضه الحلول فيه بالغرض","reading":"لكن يعرضه الحلول فيه بالعرض","status":"The final verbal form and accidental qualification are normalised provisionally; recheck small letterforms."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I196.lower.03","volume":1,"page":196,"physical_pdf_page":209,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وأجيب بأنا نختار أنه موجود ولا نسلم لزوم شيء مما ذكر لجواز أن يكون البعدان مختلفين بالماهية مشتركين في مطلق البعد فلا امتناع من اختصاص أحدهما بقبول الحركة وانقضائه واختصاص الآخر بإمكان نفوذ الشيء فيه فلا يكونان مثلين على أن اجتماعهما في المتمكن ممنوع لأن أحدهما في المتمكن والآخر فيه المتمكن","english":"The reply chooses the alternative that place is existent and denies all the stated consequences. The two extensions may differ in quiddity while sharing extension in general. One may therefore be specifically capable of movement and [cessation], and the other specifically capable of being penetrated by a thing; they need not be two instances of the same kind. Moreover, their coincidence in the occupying body is denied: one extension is in that body, while the body is in the other.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"وانقضائه","reading":"وانقضائه","status":"The noun following movement is provisional; its exact sense needs comparison."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I196.lower.04","volume":1,"page":196,"physical_pdf_page":209,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وأما العامة الخ) والأمارات المذكورات تدل على خلاف مذهبهم أيضا لكن في تسليمهم مساواة المكان للمتمكن تأمل فتأمل","english":"On “Ordinary people, however, etc.” The stated indications also oppose their view. Yet whether they accept equality between place and occupant is questionable. Consider this.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I196.lower.05","volume":1,"page":196,"physical_pdf_page":209,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإن قيل ما معنى القول بإمكانه الخ) حاصل توجيه السؤال أن اشتغال المكان بالشاغل وعدم اشتغاله إنما يتصور إذا كان موجودا وأما إذا كان وهما معدوما كما هو عند أكثر المتكلمين فلا يتصور فيه ذلك إذ لا معنى لاشتغال المعدوم بالموجود وعدم اشتغاله فلا معنى لقول إمكان خلو المكان وعدم خلوه فإن قيل سلمنا أنه لا يتصف المعدوم باشتغاله بالموجود","english":"On “If it is asked what it means to affirm its possibility, etc.” The question is this: place’s being occupied or unoccupied is conceivable only if place exists. If it is a nonexistent supposition, as most theologians hold, neither is conceivable. It makes no sense for a nonexistent to be occupied by an existent, or not occupied; consequently it makes no sense to affirm or deny the possibility of place’s being empty. If it is said: we concede that a nonexistent cannot be characterised by being occupied by an existent—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I197.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I196.upper.01","volume":1,"page":196,"physical_pdf_page":209,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ثم التفسير المذكور للمكان هو ما عليه أهل العلم والتحقيق وأما العامة فيطلقونه على ما يمنع الشيء من النزول فيجعلون الأرض مكانا للحيوان دون الهواء المحيط به ودون البعد الموجود أو الموهوم","english":"The foregoing explanations of place are those of scholars and investigators. Ordinary people use the word for what prevents a thing from descending: they make the ground the animal’s place, rather than the surrounding air or an existent or imagined extension.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I196.upper.02","volume":1,"page":196,"physical_pdf_page":209,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) اختلفوا في أن المكان سواء كان سطحا أو بعدا موجودا أو موهوما (هل يجوز خلوه عن الشاغل) فإن قيل ما معنى القول بإمكانه عند","english":"“And” they disagree over whether place—whether a surface, an existent extension or an imagined extension—“may be empty of an occupant.” If it is asked what it means to affirm its possibility on the view—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I197.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I197.lower.01","volume":1,"page":197,"physical_pdf_page":210,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"لكن لا نسلم عدم اتصافه بعدم الاشتغال به كيف وعدم اشتغال الشيء بالشاغل كما يصدق بعدم حصول الشاغل فيه يصدق بعدم ذلك الشيء في نفسه أيضا فخلو المكان إذا كان معدوما واجب فضلا عن إمكانه فحينئذ كان الواجب أن يقال لا معنى للقول بجواز خلو المكان وعدم جوازه (قلت) إنما يتم لو اعتبر عدم الاشتغال بطريق السلب وأما إذا اعتبر بطريق العدول والتحصيل كما هو المراد هنا فلا وعلى تقدير تسليمه لا ضرر فيه إذ غايته أن يجعل توجيها آخر للسؤال لكن الجواب المذكور بقوله قلنا الخ إنما يصلح جوابا على التوجيه الأول لا على هذا أيضا فإن الموافق لعبارة الشارح مد ظله هو التوجيه الأول فتدبر جدا","english":"But we do not concede that it cannot be characterised by not being occupied by it. A thing’s not being occupied by an occupant is true not only when the occupant is absent from it, but also when the thing itself does not exist. The emptiness of place, if place is nonexistent, is therefore necessary, not merely possible. One should then say that it makes no sense to affirm or deny the possibility of place’s being empty. I reply: that follows only if non-occupation is considered as a negation. It does not follow if considered as a negative qualification with a subject actually posited, which is what is intended here. Even conceding the point does no harm: at most it supplies another formulation of the question. But the reply beginning “We say, etc.” answers only the first formulation, not this one as well. The first formulation accords with the commentator’s wording—may his shade endure. Reflect very carefully.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I196.lower.final","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I197.lower.02","volume":1,"page":197,"physical_pdf_page":210,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أن يكون الجسمان بحيث الخ) أقول قد سبق أن اشتغال الموهوم بالموجود إنما هو على سبيل التوهم فافهم (قوله دفعة الخ) أي بحيث لا يكون ارتفاع أحد الجانبين قبل الآخر حتى يلزم الانفكاك","english":"On “That the two bodies can be situated so that, etc.” I say: it was explained earlier that an imagined space’s occupation by an existent is itself only a matter of supposition. Understand this. On “All at once, etc.”: so that one side does not rise before the other, producing separation [first on one side].","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I197.lower.03","volume":1,"page":197,"physical_pdf_page":210,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله سطحاهما المستويان الخ) قد يمنع إمكان أن يوجد جسم له سطح مستو ليس فيه ارتفاع وانخفاض أصلا وكذا مع كون التماس من السطحين لم يجوز أن يكون فيه أجزاء لا تتميز من المكامن كما هو عند المتكلمين","english":"On “Their level surfaces, etc.” The possibility of a body with a level surface entirely free from elevations and depressions may be disputed. Even with the two surfaces in contact, why should there not be undiscernible interstices among the parts, as the theologians hold?","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"أجزاء لا تتميز من المكامن","reading":"أجزاء لا تتميز من المكامن","status":"The precise wording describing microscopic recesses is difficult; the objection to perfectly smooth contact is clear."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I197.lower.04","volume":1,"page":197,"physical_pdf_page":210,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بل قد يخلقه الله الخ) أو يصل إليه من المنافذ والمسامات بين الأجزاء كما هو عندهم أيضا (قوله وأنت خبير الخ) تحريره هو أنه إن أريد بكونه","english":"On “Rather, God may create it, etc.” Alternatively, air may reach it through channels and pores between the parts, as they also hold. On “You are aware, etc.” The precise formulation is this: if “all at once” means—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I198.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I197.upper.01","volume":1,"page":197,"physical_pdf_page":210,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"من جعله قاصرا قلنا هو أنه يمكن أن يكون الجسمان بحيث لا يتماسان ولا يكون بينهما ما يماسهما (قيل نعم) يجوز (لأنا إذا رفعنا صفحة ملساء دفعة عن) صفحة أخرى ملساء (مثلها) حين مماس سطحيهما المستويان بحيث لا جسم حائل بينهما (لزم في أول زمان الارتفاع خلو الوسط) ضرورة فإن الهواء إنما ينتقل إليه من الأطراف ويمر بالأجزاء بالتدريج ويصل بالآخرة إلى الوسط فعند كونه على الأطراف يكون الوسط خاليا","english":"[In reply to one] who finds it inadequate, we say: [the meaning is] that two bodies can be situated so that they neither touch each other nor have between them something touching both of them. “It has been said: yes”—it is possible—“because, if we lift a smooth plate all at once from another like it,” while their level surfaces touch with no intervening body, “the middle must be empty at the first instant of separation.” Air enters from the edges, passes through the parts successively and only finally reaches the middle. While it is at the edges, the middle is therefore empty.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I196.upper.final","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Full native upper-register enlargement confirms ما يماسهما and (قيل نعم) يجوز (لأنا ...), correcting an invented لمجرد. The absence of a body touching both is the source wording, not merely an explanatory paraphrase."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I197.upper.02","volume":1,"page":197,"physical_pdf_page":210,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وهذا إلزامي مبني على ما هو عند الخصم لا برهاني فإنه عند المتكلم لا يجب انتقال الهواء إليه من الأطراف بل قد يخلقه الله تعالى فيه دفعة وأنت خبير بأنه لا يتم هذا الإلزام عليهم إلا ببيان جواز","english":"This is an argument against the opponent from his own commitments, not a demonstration: for the theologian, air need not enter from the edges, since God may create it there all at once. You are aware, however, that this argument against them is not complete without establishing the possibility of—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I198.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I198.lower.01","volume":1,"page":198,"physical_pdf_page":211,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"دفعة كونه في آن لا يقيم أصلا فلا نسلم إمكانه فإن الارتفاع حركة فتقتضي زمانا وإن أريد كون حركات جميع أجزائه معا بحيث لا يلزم منه الانفكاك فلا نسلم استلزامه للخلاء فإن الحركة لما كان لها زمان جاز أن يمر الهواء فيه من الأطراف إلى الوسط","english":"—its occurring in an instant with no duration at all, we do not concede its possibility: ascent is motion and therefore requires time. If it means that all the parts move together, without one separating before the others, we do not concede that this entails a void. Since motion has a duration, air may pass during it from the edges to the middle.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I197.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I198.lower.02","volume":1,"page":198,"physical_pdf_page":211,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وأجيب بجواز أن يبقى الخ) ويجوز أن ينفذ الهواء من المسام وإن بولغ في شدها كما هو عندهم أيضا","english":"On “The reply is that a little air may remain, etc.” Air may also penetrate through the pores, however tightly they are closed, as they likewise maintain.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I198.lower.03","volume":1,"page":198,"physical_pdf_page":211,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله قوامه نصف قوام الملاء الأول الخ) أي يكون ملاء أرق من الملاء الأول في نسبة زمان حركة الملاء إلى زمان حركة الملاء الأول كما يكون زمان الخلاء نصف زمان الملاء الأول وكذلك يكون قوام الملاء","english":"On “Whose density is half that of the first plenum, etc.” That is, a plenum thinner than the first, whose [relative] density corresponds to the ratio between the duration of motion through this plenum and that through the first. Just as motion through the void takes half the time of motion through the first plenum, the density of the thinner plenum is—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I199.lower.01","editorial":"This reproduces the argument’s proposed proportionality, not a modern physical law.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I198.upper.01","volume":1,"page":198,"physical_pdf_page":211,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الارتفاع دفعة أي في آن والحكيم يمنعه فإن الارتفاع حركة وكل حركة عنده في زمان إذ لابد أن تكون الحركة على مسافة منقسمة وقطع بعضها متقدم على قطع كلها فوقعها إنما هو في زمان وأنه منقسم إلى غير النهاية ففي زمان ارتفاعها ينتقل الهواء من طرفها إلى الوسط فلا يلزم خلوه","english":"—the plate’s rising all at once, that is, in an instant. The philosopher denies this, because rising is motion, and every motion, on his account, occurs in time. Motion must traverse a divisible distance; traversing part precedes traversing the whole. Its occurrence is therefore temporal, and that time is divisible without end. During the plate’s ascent, air can move from its edge to the middle, so the middle need not be empty.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I197.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I198.upper.02","volume":1,"page":198,"physical_pdf_page":211,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) لأنا (إذا رفعنا أحد جانبي الزق) اللاصق أحد جانبيه بالآخر بحيث لا يكون بينهما هواء (المسدود الرأس والمسـام جميعها عن الجانب الآخر خلا جوفه) وأجيب بجواز أن يبقى في جانبي الزق قليل هواء ويتخلل عند الارتفاع","english":"“And” because “if we lift one side of a waterskin”—whose sides adhere with no air between them, “its mouth and all its pores sealed—away from the other side, its interior becomes empty.” The reply is that a little air may remain between the sides of the waterskin and spread through it as one side is raised.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I198.upper.03","volume":1,"page":198,"physical_pdf_page":211,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وقيل لا) يجوز الخلو عن الشاغل (وإلا لزم تساوي زمان الحركة مع وجود المعاوق و) زمان تلك الحركة (مع عدمه) واللازم ظاهر البطلان بيان الملازمة (فيما إذا فرضنا حركة جسم في فرسخ خلاء) ولا محالة يكون في زمان (وليكن ساعة و) فرضنا حركة (أخرى لذلك الجسم مثلها في القوة في فرسخ ملاء) ولا محالة تكون في زمان أكثر لوجود المعاوق (وليكن ساعتين و) فرضنا حركة (أخرى لذلك الجسم مثلها بتلك القوة في فرسخ ملاء قوامه نصف قوام الملاء الأول فيكون زمان الحركة في هذا الملاء الأرق ساعة) أيضا (ضرورة أن المسافة والمتحرك والقوة المحركة إذا اتحدت يكون تفاوت الزمان","english":"“It has also been said: no,” a space cannot be empty of an occupant. “Otherwise, the duration of motion with a resisting medium would equal its duration without one,” which is plainly impossible. The entailment is explained by supposing a body to move “through one parasang of void.” It must take some time: “let it be one hour.” Suppose “another” motion of that same body, with the same “force, through one parasang of plenum.” Because of the resisting medium it must take longer: “let it be two hours.” Now suppose “another” motion of the body, “with that force, through one parasang of a plenum whose density is half that of the first. The duration of motion in this thinner plenum will be one hour” as well. “For, necessarily, when distance, moving body and moving force are the same, the difference in duration—”","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I199.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I199.lower.01","volume":1,"page":199,"physical_pdf_page":212,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الأرق نصف قوام الملاء الأول على ما صرح به في شرح المقاصد وقد يمنع إمكان أن تكون نسبة القوامين على نسبة الزمانين وإنما يتم ذلك لو لم ينته القوام الأول إلى ملاء أرق منه وهو ممنوع","english":"—half that of the first plenum, as explicitly stated in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. The possibility of the two densities’ standing in the same ratio as the two durations may be disputed. The argument holds only if the first density does not reach a limit beyond which there is no thinner plenum; that is not conceded.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I198.lower.03","readings":[{"locus":"لو لم ينته القوام الأول إلى ملاء أرق منه","reading":"لو لم ينته القوام الأول إلى ملاء أرق منه","status":"The printed limit-clause is compressed; the English states its apparent limiting-density objection provisionally."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I199.lower.02","volume":1,"page":199,"physical_pdf_page":212,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله في القلة والكثرة الخ) إنما يتم ذلك لو انقسم المعاوقة بانقسام القوام بحيث تكون سارية في الجسم منقسمة بانقسامه فيكون جزء المعاوق معاوقا وهو ممنوع لم لا يجوز أن تكون متوقفة على قدر من القوام بحيث لا توجد بدونه","english":"On “In being shorter or longer, etc.” This holds only if resistance divides in proportion to density, permeating the body and dividing with it, so that a part of the resisting medium is itself resistant. That is not conceded. Why may resistance not depend on a certain amount of density, below which it does not occur?","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I199.lower.03","volume":1,"page":199,"physical_pdf_page":212,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله واعترض بأنه لا يلزم من كون الزمانين الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد إنه لا يلزم من كون المعاوقة على نسبة الزمانين الخ انتهى أقول أراد بالزمانين زمان الخلاء وزمان الملاء الأول كما سبق منه آنفا فالشارح مد ظله إن أراد بهما ذينك الزمانين فلا وجه لتغيير العبارة بل فيه تأمل كما لا يخفى وإن أراد بهما زماني الملاءين فلا شك أن كونهما على نسبة المعاوقين بأن يكون زمان الأرق نصف زمان الآخر كما أن معاوقته نصف معاوقة الآخر لا يستلزم كون زمان قليل المعاوق مساويا لزمان عدمه بالضرورة فيكون قوله لا يلزم الخ لغوا وكذا قوله وإنما يلزم لو لم يكن الخ تأمل كما لا يخفى","english":"On “The objection is that the two times’ being proportionate, etc.” Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid says: it does not follow from the resistance’s being proportionate to the two times, etc. End of quotation. I say: “the two times” there means the time in the void and that in the first plenum, as stated just before. If the commentator—may his shade endure—means those same two times, there is no reason to alter the wording; indeed the alteration is questionable. If he means the times in the two plenums, their being proportionate to the two resistances—so that the thinner medium takes half the time, just as its resistance is half the other’s—plainly does not entail equality between the time with small resistance and the time without resistance. “It does not entail, etc.” would then be superfluous; likewise “That would follow only if, etc.” requires consideration, as is evident.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I199.lower.04","volume":1,"page":199,"physical_pdf_page":212,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"اللهم إلا أن يقال أراد زماني الملاءين لكون المقصود من معارضته هو أنه لا يلزم من كون تفاوت الزمانين على حسب تفاوت المعاوقين ذلك التساوي فتدبر جدا فإنه دقيق","english":"Unless one says that he means the times in the two plenums, and that his objection is directed to the following point: the difference between the times corresponding to the difference between the resistances does not entail the equality in question. Reflect very carefully, for this is subtle.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I199.lower.05","volume":1,"page":199,"physical_pdf_page":212,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وأما إذا كانت الحركة بنفسها الخ) قال المراد بنفس الحركة حركة ذلك الجسم بتلك القوة من غير اعتبار معاوقة المخروق لا ماهية الحركة","english":"On “But if motion itself, etc.” He says: “motion itself” means the motion of that body by that force, without considering resistance from the traversed medium, not the quiddity of motion—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I200.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I199.upper.01","volume":1,"page":199,"physical_pdf_page":212,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"في القلة والكثرة بحسب تفاوت المعاوق فيهما فيلزم تساوي زمان حركة ذي المعاوق أعني الجسم في الملاء الأرق وزمان تحرك عديم المعاوق وهو ما إذا كان في الخلاء واعترض بأنه لا يلزم من كون الزمانين على نسبة المعاوقين أن يكون زمان قليل المعاوق مساويا لزمان عدمه وإنما يلزم لو لم يكن الزمان إلا بإزاء المعاوقة وأما إذا كانت الحركة بنفسها تستدعي شيئا من الزمان كالساعة المفروضة في الخلاء فإذا المعاوق القليل يكون ساعة بإزاء نفس الحركة كما في الخلاء ونصف ساعة بإزاء المعاوقة التي هي نصف المعاوقة الكثيرة التي يقع ساعة بإزائها","english":"—“in being shorter or longer follows the difference between the resisting media.” Thus the time taken by a motion with resistance—the body’s motion through the thinner plenum—would equal the time taken without resistance, namely in the void. The objection is that the two times’ being proportionate to the two resistances does not entail that the time with a smaller resistance equals the time without resistance. That would follow only if time were due solely to resistance. But if motion itself requires some time, such as the hour posited for motion through the void, then with the smaller resistance there will be one hour for motion itself, as in the void, and half an hour for the resistance: half the greater resistance, for which one hour was assigned.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I198.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I199.upper.02","volume":1,"page":199,"physical_pdf_page":212,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومن","english":"“Among—”","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I200.upper.01","editorial":"The printed lemma is divided at the page boundary.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I200.lower.01","volume":1,"page":200,"physical_pdf_page":213,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"من حيث هي وإلا لورد الاعتراض بأنها لو اقتضت قدرا من الزمان لزم ثبوت ذلك القدر لكل من جزئيات الحركة لامتناع تخلف مقتضى الماهية عنها واللازم باطل كما في الحركة المفروضة في جزء من ذلك القدر من الزمان ولا الحركة المجردة من السرعة والبطء وإلا لورد أن الحركة يمتنع أن توجد إلا على حد من السرعة والبطء لأنها لا محالة تكون في مسافة وزمان ينقسم كل منهما إلى لا النهاية فإذا فرضنا وقوع أخرى تقطع تلك المسافة في نصف ذلك الزمان تكون الأولى أبطأ منها أو ضعفه تكون أسرع فالحركة المجردة منها لا توجد وما لا يوجد لا يستدعي شيئا من الزمان فليتأمل","english":"—considered as such. Otherwise one could object that, if the quiddity of motion required a certain amount of time, every individual motion would require that amount, since what a quiddity entails cannot be absent from its instances. That consequence is false: consider a motion occurring in part of the supposed time. Nor does he mean motion abstracted from speed and slowness. One could then object that motion cannot exist without some degree of speed or slowness, since it necessarily occurs over a distance and in a time, each divisible without end. If another motion covers that distance in half the time, the first is slower; if in twice the time, the first is faster. Motion abstracted from both does not exist, and what does not exist does not require any time. Let this be considered.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I199.lower.05","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Native-source enlargement visibly includes لا: unending divisibility. The previously flagged omission was a transcription error."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I200.lower.02","volume":1,"page":200,"physical_pdf_page":213,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لما أنه يعم الماديات الخ) وذلك لعدم المعقول والنفوس والمقدار للمكان عند من يجعله بعدا موجودا فافهم","english":"On “Because it includes material and immaterial things, etc.” This concerns [probably: the number of] the intellects and souls, and the magnitude of place for those who make it an existent extension. Understand this.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","readings":[{"locus":"لعدم المعقول والنفوس","reading":"Probably لعدد العقول والنفوس","status":"The printed small letterforms remain problematic. The proposed sense “number of intellects and souls” is explicit conjecture; it is not silently substituted as established Arabic."}],"proof_notes":["The ending is securely بعدا موجودا, not موهوما: existent extension, not imagined extension. The earlier short expression remains an open reading."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I200.upper.01","volume":1,"page":200,"physical_pdf_page":213,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"أماراته) أي من أمارات امتناع الخلاء أنه لو وجد لزم انتفاء أمور نشاهدها مثل (ارتفاع اللحم في المحجمة) فإنه عند انجذاب الهواء يتبعه اللحم لئلا يلزم الخلاء (و) ارتفاع (الماء في الأنبوبة) التي في الماص إذا انغمس أحد طرفيها في الماء ومص الطرف الآخر مع اقتضاء طبعه النزول دون الارتفاع فذلك لأن سطح الهواء ملازم لسطح الماء لامتناع الخلاء فإذا ارتفع سطح الهواء بالمص تبعه سطح الماء (وعدم نزول الماء من ثقبة) ضيقة في أسفل (الكوز المسدود الرأس) لئلا يبقى حيز الماء خاليا ونزوله عند فتح رأسه لدخول الهواء","english":"—“its indications,” that is, indications of the impossibility of a void, are that a void’s existence would require the absence of phenomena that we observe. These include “the rising of flesh in a cupping glass”: when the air is drawn out, the flesh follows it, so that no void results. “And” the rising of “water in a tube” when one end is immersed in water and suction is applied at the other, although water’s nature requires descent rather than ascent. This is because, on the impossibility of a void, the surface of the air remains contiguous with the surface of the water; when suction raises the air’s surface, the water follows. Another example is “water’s not descending through a” narrow “hole” in the bottom of “a jar whose mouth is sealed,” lest the water’s place be left empty, and its descending when the mouth is opened and air enters.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I199.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I200.upper.02","volume":1,"page":200,"physical_pdf_page":213,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وأجيب بأنه يجوز أن يكون ذلك لأسباب أخر فإن غاية هذه الأمور لزومها لانتفاء الخلاء واللازم قد يكون أعم فلا يصح الاستدلال بوجوده على وجود الملزوم (و) قد عرفت مما تلوناه أن (المعترض مستظهر من الجانبين) فلا تكن من الغافلين","english":"The reply is that these phenomena may have other causes. At most, they necessarily accompany the absence of a void; but a necessary concomitant may be more general, so its presence does not establish the presence of what entails it. “And,” as you will have recognised from what we have recounted, “the objector has the advantage on both sides.” Do not be inattentive.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Focused native-image crop confirms المعترض مستظهر من الجانبين. The first-pass claim that the passage favoured opponents of a void was incorrect; the source grants the objector an advantage against both sides."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I200.upper.03","volume":1,"page":200,"physical_pdf_page":213,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(فصل في الكيف) قدم مباحثه على سائر المقولات لأنه أعم وجودا من جميعها إذ منه المحسوسات التي هي أظهر الموجودات إلا أنه قدم الكم عليه لما أنه يعم الماديات والمجردات فإن قيل المجردات متصفة بالعلم وهو من مقولة الكيف","english":"“A chapter on quality.” Its discussions precede those of the remaining categories because quality is more widespread in existence than all of them: it includes the objects of sense, the most manifest existents. Quantity nevertheless preceded it because quantity includes both material and immaterial things. If it is objected that immaterial things possess knowledge, which belongs to the category of quality—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I201.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I201.lower.01","volume":1,"page":201,"physical_pdf_page":214,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهو عرض لا يقبل الخ) لا خفاء في أنه لا طريق إلى تعريف الأجناس العالية إلا بالرسوم إذ لا يتصور لها جنس وهو ظاهر ولا فصل لأن التركيب من أمرين متساويين محال على ما تحقق في محله","english":"On “It is an accident that does not admit, etc.” It is evident that the highest genera can be defined only descriptively. They cannot have a genus, which is clear, nor a differentia, because composition from two coextensive constituents is impossible, as established in its proper place.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I201.lower.02","volume":1,"page":201,"physical_pdf_page":214,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فخرج الجوهر) أي بقيد العرض (قوله والكم) أي بقيد عدم القسمة وسائر الأعراض أي بعدم قبول النسبة (قوله ومن جعل النقطة) أي جعلهما من الأمور المتحققة زاد في هذا التعريف قيدا لإخراجهما ومن جعلهما من الأمور الاعتبارية كالمصنف لا حاجة له في ذلك إلى ذلك لعدم دخولهما في العرض","english":"On “This excludes substance”: by the qualification “accident.” On “And quantity”: by not admitting division. “The remaining accidents” are excluded by not admitting relation. On “Those who make the point, etc.”: those who regard the point and unity as things actually realised add a qualification to exclude them. Those who regard them as matters of consideration, as the author does, have no need of that addition, since they do not fall under accident.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I201.lower.03","volume":1,"page":201,"physical_pdf_page":214,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولا يخرج من التعريف) أي بقيد عدم القبول بقوله لذاته لئلا يخرج منه التعريف العلم الخ","english":"On “It is not excluded from the definition”: that is, from the qualification concerning non-admission, owing to the expression “in itself,” so that knowledge, etc., is not excluded from the definition.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"لئلا يخرج منه التعريف العلم","reading":"لئلا يخرج منه التعريف العلم","status":"Awkward visible syntax; the explanatory point concerns division according to the object rather than knowledge in itself."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I201.lower.04","volume":1,"page":201,"physical_pdf_page":214,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله واعترض بأن من الكيفيات الخ) الاعتراض والجواب مذكوران في شرح المقاصد وحاصل الاعتراض ما يفهم من حاشية منقولة منه هنا وهو أن العلم بمعنى الصورة الحاصلة من الشيء في العقل يتوقف تعقله على تعقل ذلك الشيء مع أنه جعلوه من الكيف وهو لا يقبل النسبة وحينئذ في انطباق الجواب المذكور على دفع الاعتراض تأمل إذ الظاهر فيه حينئذ أن يقال المراد من قبول النسبة المنفي في الكيف هو توقف","english":"On “The objection is that some qualities, etc.” The objection and reply occur in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. The objection’s substance is what is understood from a gloss quoted from it here: knowledge, in the sense of the form acquired from a thing in the intellect, cannot be conceived without conceiving that thing, although they classify it as quality, which does not admit relation. Whether the stated reply answers this objection therefore requires consideration. The apparent reply would instead be that the admission of relation denied of quality means the dependence of—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I202.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I201.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":201,"physical_pdf_page":214,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فليس من الكيف الخ) وهذا ادعاء يتم عند القائلين بأن علم الواجب وكذا العقول المجردة بالأشياء كلها حضوري بناء على أن كل عقل بصفاته القائمة به حاضر عند العقل الأعلى لا الصور بل نفس الذات بعينه اه منه","english":"On “It therefore does not belong to quality, etc.” This claim holds for those who maintain that the Necessary’s knowledge of all things, and likewise the knowledge possessed by the separate intellects, is knowledge by presence. They base this on each intellect’s being present to the higher intellect together with the attributes inhering in it: not its forms, but its very essence itself. End; “from him.”","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The printed signature منه is not a personal identification. “Higher intellect” is a provisional reading of the short damaged phrase.","readings":[{"locus":"عند العقل الأعلى","reading":"عند العقل الأعلى","status":"The small final words need enlarged proof; do not infer a personal author from the signature."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I201.upper.01","volume":1,"page":201,"physical_pdf_page":214,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فيعمها أيضا أجيب بأن علم المجردات حضوري فليس من الكيف وهو (عرض لا يقبل لذاته قسمة ولا نسبة) فخرج الجوهر والكم وسائر الأعراض النسبية ومن جعل النقطة والوحدة من الأعراض زاد قيد عدم اقتضائه اللاقسمة احترازا عنهما ولا يخرج من التعريف العلم بالمركب أو البسيط حيث يقتضي القسمة نظرا إلى المتعلق","english":"[The definition would] therefore include them as well. The reply is that the knowledge of immaterial beings is knowledge by presence, and therefore does not belong to quality. [Quality] is “an accident that does not, in itself, admit division or relation.” This excludes substance, quantity and the remaining relational accidents. Those who make the point and unity accidents add the qualification that it does not require indivisibility, to exclude those two. Knowledge of something composite or simple is not excluded from the definition when it entails division in consideration of its object.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I200.upper.final","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I201.upper.02","volume":1,"page":201,"physical_pdf_page":214,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"واعترض بأن من الكيفيات ما يتوقف تعقله على تعقل شيء آخر كالعلم وأجيب بأنه ليس توقفا وإنما هو استلزام أو استتباع بمعنى أن تصوره يستعقب تصور متعلقه بخلاف النسبيات فإنها لا تتصور إلا بعد تصور المنسوب","english":"The objection is that some qualities, such as knowledge, cannot be conceived without conceiving something else. The reply is that this is not dependence, but implication or entailment: conceiving knowledge is followed by conceiving its object. Relational things, by contrast, cannot be conceived until the related [subject]—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I202.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I202.lower.01","volume":1,"page":202,"physical_pdf_page":215,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"حصوله على حصول الغير لا توقف تصوره على تصور الغير الذي هو الاستلزام والاستتباع بالمعنى المذكور وإن توقف تصوره على غيره كما صرح به في شرح المقاصد لكن لا يتوقف حصوله على حصول الغير وإلا لامتنع العلم بالمعدومات نعم العلم بمعنى الإضافة وكذا سائر الأمور النسبية يتوقف حصولها لتصورها على الغير ولكن ليست من الكيف اللهم إلا أن يقال يمكن تحرير الجواب المذكور على وجه ينطبق على ما ذكرناه فحينئذ يندفع ما كاد أن يشتبه على بعض الناظرين","english":"—its occurrence upon the occurrence of something else, not the dependence of conceiving it upon conceiving something else, which is implication or entailment in the sense mentioned. Although conceiving knowledge depends upon something else, as explicitly stated in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, its occurrence does not depend upon that other’s occurrence; otherwise knowledge of nonexistent things would be impossible. Knowledge in the sense of a relation, however, like other relational things, depends upon another thing for its occurrence [and conception]; but it is not a quality. Unless one says that the stated reply can be formulated so as to accord with what we have explained; in that case, what nearly caused some readers confusion is resolved.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I201.lower.04","readings":[{"locus":"حصولها لتصورها","reading":"حصولها لتصورها","status":"The conjunction between occurrence and conception is unclear in the print; English marks the second term as a contextual reading."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I202.lower.02","volume":1,"page":202,"physical_pdf_page":215,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بخلاف مثل العلم الخ) قال في الحاشية أي بمعنى الصورة الحاصلة لا بمعنى الإضافة والانفعال","english":"On “Unlike such things as knowledge, etc.” The gloss says: that is, knowledge in the sense of the acquired form, not in the sense of relation or being affected.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I202.lower.03","volume":1,"page":202,"physical_pdf_page":215,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ليس بحسب اشتراك اللفظ الخ) حاصله أنه لما ذكروا أن الحرارات المذكورة أنواع مختلفة يتوهم أن إطلاق الحرارة عليها بحسب الاشتراك اللفظي وليس كذلك فإن الحرارة معنى واحد يعم الجميع فإنها هي الكيفية المحسوسة التي تحدث الخفة والميل المصعد ثم تترتب عليه بحسب اختلاف القوابل آثار مختلفة كجمع المتماثلات وتفريق المختلفات فإطلاقها عليها بالاشتراك المعنوي","english":"On “It is not equivocal usage, etc.” In summary: since they describe the aforementioned heats as different species, one might suppose that the term “heat” applies to them equivocally. It does not: heat is one meaning common to all, the sensible quality that produces lightness and upward inclination. Depending upon the differences among recipients, different effects follow from it, such as bringing similar things together and separating dissimilar things. The term therefore applies to these by a shared meaning—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I203.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I202.upper.01","volume":1,"page":202,"physical_pdf_page":215,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"والمنسوب إليه وذلك لكون النسبة نفس حقيقتها أو داخلة فيها بخلاف مثل العلم (وأقسامه بحسب الاستقراء أربعة) القسم (الأول) الكيفيات (المحسوسات) قدمها لأنها أظهر الأقسام الأربعة ثم أنواع الكيفيات المحسوسة خمسة بحسب الحواس الخمس الظاهرة النوع الأول الملموسات المسماة بأوائل المحسوسات قيل وجه التسمية أن القوة اللامسة تعم جميع الحيوانات ولا يخلو عنها حيوان بخلاف سائر الحواس الظاهرة والعموم مقدم على الخصوص","english":"—and the thing to which it is related have been conceived. This is because relation constitutes their very reality or enters into it, unlike such things as knowledge. “Its divisions, according to induction, are four.” “The first” comprises “sensible” qualities, placed first because they are the most manifest of the four divisions. Sensible qualities have five kinds, corresponding to the five external senses. The first kind is tangible qualities, called the primary sensibles. The proposed reason for this name is that the faculty of touch is common to all animals: no animal lacks it, unlike the other external senses; the general is prior to the particular.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I201.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I202.upper.02","volume":1,"page":202,"physical_pdf_page":215,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وأصول الملموسات) أربعة (الحرارة والبرودة والرطوبة واليبوسة) لثبوتها للبسايط العنصرية وتحصل المركبات منها بتوسط المزاج المتفرع عن هذه الأربعة ثم إطلاق الحرارة كما في شرح المقاصد على حرارة النار والحرارة الغريزية والفائضة من الأجرام السماوية والحادثة بالحركة ليس بحسب اشتراك اللفظ على ما يتوهم لأنها مفهوم واحد وهو الكيفية المحسوسة وإن كانت الحرارات متخالفة بالحقيقة فافهم","english":"“The primary tangible qualities” are four: “heat, cold, moisture and dryness.” They belong to the simple elemental bodies, and their occurrence in composites is mediated by temperament, which results from those four. As explained in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, applying “heat” to the heat of fire, innate heat, heat emanating from celestial bodies and heat generated by movement is not equivocal usage, as might be supposed: heat is one concept, namely the sensible quality, even though these heats differ in their realities. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I203.lower.01","volume":1,"page":203,"physical_pdf_page":216,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ولكن هل هو بطريق التواطؤ أو التشكيك ففيه تردد يأتي تفصيل ذلك ولعل الأمر بالفهم للإشارة إلى ما ذكر","english":"—but whether that shared meaning is predicated uniformly or with variation in degree remains disputed; its details will follow. Perhaps the injunction to understand points to what has been mentioned.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I202.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I203.lower.02","volume":1,"page":203,"physical_pdf_page":216,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله واختلاف المفهوم الخ) حاصله على ما يفيده السياق هو أن اختلاف المعنى بحسب توهم الاشتراك اللفظي إنما هو في لفظ الحار المشتق من الحرارة دون المشتق كما سبق وذلك لأن معنى الحار هو ما يقوم به الحرارة وقد يطلق على ما يحدث الحرارة وإن لم تقم به الحرارة فقد اختلف المعنى المطلق عليه لفظ الحار فلا بأس في أن تكون مشتركا كاللفظين بخلاف لفظ الحرارة فإن المعنى الذي تطلق هي عليه واحد فلا يتأتى فيها الاشتراك اللفظي","english":"On “The difference in meaning, etc.” As the context indicates, the difference in meaning that suggests equivocity concerns the word “hot,” derived from “heat,” rather than [the term from which it is] derived, as explained above. “Hot” means that in which heat inheres, but it can also be applied to what produces heat although heat does not inhere in it. The meanings to which “hot” applies therefore differ, and there is no difficulty in its being equivocal, as though it were two expressions. “Heat,” by contrast, applies to one meaning, so equivocity does not arise in it.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"دون المشتق","reading":"دون المشتق [منه]","status":"The necessary contrast is the derived word versus its source; منه is an explicitly bracketed contextual supplement, not an asserted visible word."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I203.lower.03","volume":1,"page":203,"physical_pdf_page":216,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إن قيل تعدد المعنى لا ينحصر في الاشتراك بل يحتمل الحقيقة والمجاز بل هو الظاهر هنا على ما صرح به في شرح المقاصد حيث قال وهل هي في كل من الكواكب والدواء والغذاء صفة مسماة بالحرارة كالكيفية المحسوسة في النار أو ذلك توسع وإطلاق","english":"If it is objected that multiple meanings need not involve equivocity but may instead involve literal and figurative usage—and indeed that seems the apparent position here, according to the explicit statement in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: is there in each of the stars, medicines and foods an attribute called heat, like the sensible quality in fire, or is this an extended usage and an application—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I204.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I203.upper.01","volume":1,"page":203,"physical_pdf_page":216,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) اختلاف المفهوم إنما هو في الحار إذ (قد يقال الحار لما يحدث الحرارة) وإن لم يقم به معنى يسمى بالحرارة كما يقال لما يقوم به هذا المعنى (إما بسبب ملاقاة البدن) الحيواني كالأغذية والأدوية حتى يظهر منه الحرارة في بدن الحيوان (أولا) بسببها (كالأجرام السماوات) النيرة التي تفيض منها الحرارة","english":"“And” the difference in meaning concerns “hot,” since “a thing may be called hot because it produces heat,” even when no feature called heat inheres in it, just as the term is applied to what does possess that feature. It may produce heat “through contact with the” animal “body,” as foods and drugs do, until heat appears in the animal’s body, “or not” through such contact, “as with the” luminous “celestial bodies,” from which heat emanates.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I203.upper.02","volume":1,"page":203,"physical_pdf_page":216,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وأما الحرارة الغريزية التي بها قوام الحياة) للحيوان (فقيل نارية) خرجت عن مزاجها واستفادت بالمزج مع سائر العناصر مزاجا معتدلا حصل به التئام تام بين أجزاء المركب (وقيل سماوية) لأن العناصر إذا امتزجت وانكسرت سورة كيفياتها حصل للمركب نوع وحدة وبساطة بها يناسب البسائط السماوية ففاضت عليه حرارة غريزية بها قوام الحياة (وقيل مخالفة لهما) لاختصاصها بمعاوقة الحرارة الغريبة ومدافعتها حتى أن السموم الحارة لا يدفعها إلا الحرارة الغريزية فإنها آلة للطبيعة في دفع ضرر الحرارة الواردة بتحريك الروح إلى دفعه","english":"“As for the innate heat by which” an animal’s “life subsists, some call it fiery”: it has departed from its [original] temperament and, through blending with the other elements, acquired a balanced temperament that establishes a thorough union among the composite’s parts. “Others call it celestial”: when the elements mix and the force of their qualities is moderated, the composite acquires a kind of unity and simplicity through which it resembles the celestial simples. Innate heat, through which life subsists, then emanates upon it. “Others hold it to differ from both,” because it is specifically able to resist and repel foreign heat. Indeed, hot poisons are repelled only by innate heat, which is nature’s instrument for counteracting the harm of incoming heat by moving the spirit to repel it.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I203.upper.03","volume":1,"page":203,"physical_pdf_page":216,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومنها) أي من الكيفيات الملموسة (الاعتماد بمعنى","english":"“Among them”—the tangible qualities—“is inclination, in the sense of—”","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I204.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I204.lower.01","volume":1,"page":204,"physical_pdf_page":217,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"للحرارة على ما منه الحرارة وإن لم يقم به معنى الحرارة فيه تردد انتهى (قلت) ذلك لا يمنع توهم الاشتراك أو نقول المراد بالاشتراك هنا تعدد المعنى أعم من أن يكون بطريق الحقيقة أولا أو نقول ليس كلا المعنيين للفظ الحار حقيقتان في الاصطلاح إن قيل لما لم يتحقق عنده عدم قيام الحرارة بالأمور المذكورة بل كان قيام الحرارة بها محتملا كما أن فيه تردد احتمل أن إطلاق الحار عليها إنما هو من جهة القيام لا من جهة الأحداث فلا تعدد لمعنى المشتق أيضا قلت لعلهم اعتبروا في إطلاق الحار على مثل الدواء حيثية الإحداث دون القيام ففي قوله وإن لم يقم به وإن لم يستقر فيه حيثية القيام سواء قام به أولا وذلك كاللامسة تطلق على القوة التي تحدث لمسة فقطعنا أنه لا ينبغي أن يبحث عن تحقيقه","english":"—of “heat” to what heat comes from, even if no heat inheres in it? This remains uncertain. End of quotation. I reply: that does not prevent equivocity from being supposed. Alternatively, “equivocity” here means multiple meanings, whether literal or otherwise; or we may say that the two meanings of “hot” are not both literal in technical usage. If it is objected that the absence of heat from these things has not been established, since its inherence in them remains possible and uncertain, then “hot” might be applied because heat inheres in them, not because they produce it; the derived word would therefore have no multiplicity of meanings either. I reply: perhaps in calling a medicine hot they consider its producing heat rather than heat’s inhering in it. “Even if it does not inhere” would then mean that the aspect of inherence has not been settled, whether it does inhere or not. This resembles applying “tactile” to a faculty that produces touch [the remainder of this comparison is difficult].","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I203.lower.03","readings":[{"locus":"ففي قوله وإن لم يقم به وإن لم يستقر فيه","reading":"ففي قوله وإن لم يقم به وإن لم يستقر فيه","status":"Compressed explanatory syntax; the distinction between production and inherence is explicit."},{"locus":"فقطعنا أنه لا ينبغي أن يبحث عن تحقيقه","reading":"فقطعنا أنه لا ينبغي أن يبحث عن تحقيقه","status":"The closing comparison requires enlarged rereading. Its uncertain wording is not silently asserted as a completed argument."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I204.lower.02","volume":1,"page":204,"physical_pdf_page":217,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله المدافعة المحسوسة الخ) وهي مدافعة الجسم لما يمنعه من الحركة إلى الجهة (قوله هو غير الحركة الخ) وكذا هو غير الطبيعة لأنه ينعدم مع بقائها كما في الجسم الساكن في حيزه الطبيعي","english":"On “Sensible resistance, etc.” It is the body’s resistance to what prevents its movement in the relevant direction. On “It is not motion, etc.” It is likewise not nature, because it can be absent while nature remains, as in a body resting in its natural place.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I204.lower.03","volume":1,"page":204,"physical_pdf_page":217,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فليس بمحسوس الخ) فهو كيفية مغايرة للطبيعة وليست من الكيفيات المحسوسة لكن لم يتحقق أنها من أي قسم من أقسام الكيفية كذا قالوا","english":"On “It is not sensible, etc.” It is thus a quality distinct from nature and not one of the sensible qualities. Yet the division of quality to which it belongs has not been established. That is what they say.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I204.lower.04","volume":1,"page":204,"physical_pdf_page":217,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وقد يجعل أنواعه الخ) قال إن الاعتماد الطبيعي الذي فيه الاختلاف بالحقيقة إنما","english":"On “Its species are sometimes made, etc.” He says: the natural inclination whose differences are real is only—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I205.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I204.upper.01","volume":1,"page":204,"physical_pdf_page":217,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"المدافعة المحسوسة) وثبوته ضروري فإن من حمل حجرا ثقيلا أحس منه اعتمادا وميلا إلى جهة السفل ومن وضع يده على زق منفوخ فيه مسكن تحت الماء أحس ميله إلى العلو ثم هو غير الحركة لأنه يوجد عند السكون كما في مثال الزق فإنا نجد فيه مدافعة صاعدة وفي الحجر المسكن في الهواء مدافعة نازلة وأما الاعتماد بمعنى ما يوجب للجسم تلك المدافعة فليس بمحسوس","english":"—“sensible resistance.” Its existence is immediately evident: anyone carrying a heavy stone senses its inclination and tendency downwards; anyone placing a hand upon an inflated waterskin held beneath water senses its tendency upwards. It is not motion, since it exists during rest: in the waterskin just described we find upward resistance, and in a stone held still in the air downward resistance. Inclination in the sense of that which produces this resistance in the body, however, is not sensible.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I203.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I204.upper.02","volume":1,"page":204,"physical_pdf_page":217,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وقد يجعل أنواعه) بحسب الحركات في الجهات الست (ستة) وحصر الجهات في الست (بحسب العرف) اعتبره العوام من حال الإنسان في أن له رأسا وقدما وظهرا وبطنا ويمينا وشمالا والخواص من حال الجسم في أن له أبعادا ثلاثة متقاطعة على زوايا قوائم ولكل بعد طرفين وأما بحسب الحقيقة","english":"“Its species are sometimes made six,” corresponding to movements in the six directions. Restricting directions to six is “according to customary usage.” Ordinary people derive them from the human condition: a person has a head and feet, a back and belly, and a right and left. Specialists derive them from the body’s having three dimensions intersecting at right angles, with two extremities for each dimension. In reality, however—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I205.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I205.lower.01","volume":1,"page":205,"physical_pdf_page":218,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"هو الصاعد والهابط اللذان لا يتبدلان أصلا كما يأتي بخلاف سائر الجهات فإنها إضافية تتبدل كالمواجه للمشرق إذا وجه للمغرب صار قدامه خلفا وما على يمينه شمالا وبالعكس فلا تكون أنواعا مختلفة فضلا عن جعلها ستة فافهم","english":"[The natural pair] is the upward and downward directions, which do not change at all, as will be explained. The other directions, by contrast, are relative and change: if someone facing east turns to face west, what was in front becomes behind, and what was on his right becomes his left, and conversely. They are therefore not distinct species, much less six such species. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I204.lower.final","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I205.lower.02","volume":1,"page":205,"physical_pdf_page":218,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فالجهات متكثرة الخ) بتكثر ما للجسم من الأجزاء عند من يقول بالجوهر الفرد أو غير متناهية بحسب ما يفرض له من الانقسامات عند غيرهم","english":"On “The directions are multiple, etc.” They are multiple in accordance with the number of parts of the body, for those who affirm the indivisible atom; for others, they are unlimited in accordance with the divisions that can be posited in the body.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I205.lower.03","volume":1,"page":205,"physical_pdf_page":218,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهي الخفة الخ) قالوا الاعتماد بالنسبة إلى العلو يسمى خفة وإلى التحت يسمى ثقلا وليس له بالنسبة إلى سائر الجهات اسم خاص","english":"On “Namely lightness, etc.” They say: inclination with respect to the upward direction is called lightness, and with respect to the downward direction heaviness. With respect to the other directions, it has no special name.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I205.lower.04","volume":1,"page":205,"physical_pdf_page":218,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي الميل الهابط الخ) قالوا إن كلا من الثقل والخفة ثقلان مطلق ومضاف فالثقل المطلق كيفية تقتضي حركة الجسم إلى حيث ينطبق مركز ثقله على مركز العالم كما في الأرض والمضاف ما يقتضي حركته إلى المركز لكن لا ينطبق عليه كما في الماء والخفة المطلقة ما يقتضي حركة الجسم إلى المحيط لطبعه الثابت فيه كما في النار والمضافة ما يقتضيها إلى المحيط لكنها لا تبلغ إليه كما في الماء","english":"On “That is, the downward inclination, etc.” They divide both heaviness and lightness into absolute and relative. Absolute heaviness is a quality requiring a body to move to where its centre of gravity coincides with the centre of the world, as with earth. Relative heaviness requires its movement towards the centre, but without coinciding with it, as with water. Absolute lightness requires the body’s movement towards the circumference through the nature established in it, as with fire. Relative lightness requires movement towards the circumference without reaching it, as with water.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"ثقلان مطلق ومضاف","reading":"ثقلان مطلق ومضاف","status":"The visible wording is compressed; the ensuing absolute/relative division is translated, without silently correcting the Arabic."},{"locus":"لطبعه الثابت فيه","reading":"لطبعه الثابت فيه","status":"Short descriptive phrase needs a second enlarged reading."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I205.lower.05","volume":1,"page":205,"physical_pdf_page":218,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله المقارنان للطبع الخ) لعدم تبدلهما حتى لو نكس الإنسان مثلا لم يصر فوقه تحته وتحته فوقه بل صار رجلاه إلى فوق ورأسه إلى تحت (قوله لأن بينهما غاية الخلاف الخ) هذا إذا اشترط في التضاد ذلك أما إذا لم يشترط فيه غاية الخلاف فأنواعه متضادة مطلقا","english":"On “Which accompany nature, etc.” This is because they do not change. Even if a person is turned upside down, for example, his above does not become his below, or his below his above; rather, his feet are now above and his head below. On “Because the difference between them is maximal, etc.” This applies if maximal difference is a condition of contrariety. If it is not required, the kinds [of inclination] are contrary without that restriction.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I205.lower.06","volume":1,"page":205,"physical_pdf_page":218,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والفلاسفة يسمونه الميل الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد لهم قد ذكرنا ما يدل على ترددهم في أن الميل نفس المدافعة المحسوسة أو مبدؤها القريب الذي يوجد عند كون الحجر صاعدا في الهواء أو ساكنا على الأرض انتهى فتدبر","english":"On “The philosophers call it mayl, etc.” In Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid he says of them: we have already mentioned what indicates their hesitation over whether inclination is the sensible resistance itself or its proximate principle, which exists when a stone is rising through the air or resting upon the ground. End of quotation. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I205.upper.01","volume":1,"page":205,"physical_pdf_page":218,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فالجهات متكثرة و(الجهة الطبيعي) الذي لا يتبدل أصلا (منها) أي من أنواع الاعتماد (ما يكون إلى فوق وهو الخفة) يعني الميل الصاعد (أو إلى تحت وهو الثقل) أي الميل الهابط وذلك لأن الجهة الطبيعية العلو والسفل المقارنان للطبع فيكون الاعتماد الطبيعي نحو أحدهما (وهما متضادان) لأن بينهما غاية الخلاف","english":"The directions are multiple. As for the natural direction, which does not change at all: “among them”—that is, among the kinds of inclination—“is what is directed upwards, namely lightness,” meaning the upward inclination, “or downwards, namely heaviness,” meaning the downward inclination. This is because the natural directions are the up and down that accompany nature; natural inclination therefore tends towards one of them. “The two are contraries,” because the difference between them is maximal.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I204.upper.final","readings":[{"locus":"الجهة الطبيعي","reading":"الجهة الطبيعي","status":"The visible construction is awkward; the natural-versus-relative direction distinction is secure."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I205.upper.02","volume":1,"page":205,"physical_pdf_page":218,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والفلاسفة يسمونه) أي الاعتماد (الميل ويجعلونه) أقساما ثلاثة (قسريا وطبعيا وإراديا لأن مبدأه إما من خارج) عن محل الميل كالسهم المرمي إلى فوق (فقسري وإلا فإن كان مع شعور) وقصد كاعتماد الإنسان على غيره (فإرادي وإلا فطبيعي) كميل الحجر المسكن في الهواء","english":"“The philosophers call it”—inclination in the preceding terminology—“mayl, and divide it” into three kinds: “constrained, natural and voluntary. For its principle is either outside” the subject of the inclination, as with an arrow shot upwards, “in which case it is constrained; otherwise, if accompanied by awareness” and intention, as when a person leans upon another thing, “it is voluntary; otherwise it is natural,” as with the inclination of a stone held stationary in the air—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I206.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I206.lower.01","volume":1,"page":206,"physical_pdf_page":219,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إذ هو ليس من مبدأ خارج الخ) فيكون الطبيعي أعم من أن يكون على وتيرة واحدة كميل حجر مسكن في الهواء أو على وتيرة مختلفة كميل النبات إلى الجهات ومنهم من يجعل ما لا يكون على وتيرة واحدة من النفساني ويفسره بما هو يشمل الإرادي وهذا القسم فسمى الطبيعي وأما من يجعل النفساني مرادفا للإرادي والطبيعي مخصوصا بما هو على وتيرة واحدة فيجعل ميل النبات خارجا من الأقسام لكونه مركبا","english":"On “Since it does not arise from an external principle, etc.” Natural inclination is accordingly general enough to include both a uniform pattern, such as the inclination of a stone held still in the air, and a varied pattern, such as a plant’s inclination in different directions. Some assign what does not follow a uniform pattern to the psychic kind, explaining that kind so as to include both voluntary inclination and this division [which others call natural]. Those who make “psychic” synonymous with “voluntary” and restrict “natural” to a uniform pattern exclude a plant’s inclination from these divisions, because the plant is composite.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"وهذا القسم فسمى الطبيعي","reading":"وهذا القسم فسمى الطبيعي","status":"The compressed naming clause is rendered contextually in brackets; verify before a diplomatic edition."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I206.lower.02","volume":1,"page":206,"physical_pdf_page":219,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله المختصة بالكميات) كالاستقامة والانحناء (ومن المقادير والأوضاع الخ) كالطول والقصر والقرب والبعد والحركة والسكون والضحك والبكاء وما يتوهم من إبصار نحو الرطوبة واليبوسة مبني على أن يبصر ملزوماتها كالسيلان والتفتت الراجعين إلى الحركة والسكون","english":"On “Particular to quantities”: such as straightness and curvature. On “Magnitudes, positions, etc.”: such as length and shortness, nearness and distance, motion and rest, laughter and weeping. The supposed visibility of such things as moisture and dryness depends on seeing what entails them, such as flowing and crumbling, which reduce to motion and rest.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I206.lower.03","volume":1,"page":206,"physical_pdf_page":219,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كالسواد والبياض الخ) الظاهر من كلام القوم هو أن أنواع اللون مطلق السواد والبياض والحمرة وغيرها لكن المحققين على أن النوع ليس مطلقها بل هو البياض المخصوص الذي لا تتفاوت أفراده كبياض الثلج مثلا وكذا تلك السواد والحمرة وغيرهما وذلك مثل ما مر في الملموسات","english":"On “For example, blackness and whiteness, etc.” Their statements apparently make blackness, whiteness, redness and the others, taken without restriction, the species of colour. The investigators, however, hold that the species is not unrestricted whiteness but a particular whiteness whose instances do not differ in degree—for example, the whiteness of snow. The same applies to blackness, redness and the other colours. This resembles what was said concerning tangible qualities—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I207.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I206.upper.01","volume":1,"page":206,"physical_pdf_page":219,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"إلى السفل (فميل مثل النبات إلى التبرد والتزيد) في الأقطار (طبيعي) إذ هو ليس من مبدأ خارج عن محل الميل ولا من قصد وما قال من أن الطبيعي لا يكون إلا صاعدا أو هابطا فإنما هو في البسائط العنصرية","english":"[The stone’s inclination is] downwards. “Thus the inclination of something such as a plant towards cooling and increase” in its dimensions “is natural,” since it does not arise from a principle external to the subject of the inclination, nor from intention. The assertion that natural inclination is only upward or downward concerns only the simple elemental bodies.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I205.upper.02","readings":[{"locus":"التبرد والتزيد","reading":"التبرد والتزيد","status":"التبرد is the provisional visible reading; the plant’s dimensional growth is secure, but the first noun needs proof."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I206.upper.02","volume":1,"page":206,"physical_pdf_page":219,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) القسم الثاني من الكيفيات المحسوسة المبصرات قالوا (أصول المبصرات) أي أوائلها التي هي مبصرة بالذات (الألوان والأضواء) وإن كانت رؤية اللون مشروطة برؤية الضوء ومعنى كونهما مبصرين بالذات أن لكل منهما رؤية على حدة متعلقة به دون الآخر ولهذا انكشف كل منهما عند الحس انكشافا تاما بخلاف غيرهما من الكيفيات المختصة بالكميات ومن المقادير والأوضاع وغير ذلك مما لا يعد في الكيفيات المحسوسة فإنها مبصرة بتوسطهما أعني أن الرؤية المتعلقة باللون أولا وبالذات هي بعينها متعلقة بها ثانيا وبالعرض على قياس قيام الحركة بالسفينة وراكبها ولهذا لم تنكشف انكشافهما","english":"“And” the second division of sensible qualities comprises visible things. They say: “The primary visibles”—the first things visible in their own right—“are colours and lights,” although seeing colour is conditional upon seeing light. Their being visible in their own right means that each has a distinct act of vision directed to it rather than to the other. Hence each is fully disclosed to sense. Other things—qualities particular to quantities, magnitudes, positions and the like, which are not included among sensible qualities—are visible through those two. The very act of vision directed primarily and in its own right to colour is directed to these secondarily and accidentally, by analogy with motion’s belonging to a ship and to its passenger. They are therefore not disclosed in the same way as colour and light.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I206.upper.03","volume":1,"page":206,"physical_pdf_page":219,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ولكل منهما أنواع) كالسواد والبياض","english":"“Each of the two has species”: for example, blackness and whiteness—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I207.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I207.lower.01","volume":1,"page":207,"physical_pdf_page":220,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"من أن النوع منها ليس الحرارة مثلا بل هو الحرارة المخصوصة التي تكون في أفرادها على السواء كحرارة النار ثم إن مطلق البياض مثلا كما أنه ليس نوعا ليس جنسا لأنه مقول بالتشكيك وهو لا يكون إلا عارضا كما قالوا من أن التفاوت في الماهية وذاتياتها ممتنع واستدلوا عليه بأن الأمر الذي به يتحقق التفاوت حين يوجد في الأشد دون الأضعف إن لم يكن داخلا في الماهية لم يتحقق التفاوت فيها وإن كان داخلا لم يتحقق اشتراك الأضعف مع الأشد في الماهية لانتفاء بعض أجزائها فيه","english":"[As previously explained,] the species of a tangible quality is not heat as such, for example, but the particular heat present equally in its instances, such as the heat of fire. Unrestricted whiteness, for example, is no more a genus than it is a species, because it is predicated with variation in degree; what is so predicated can only be incidental. This accords with their assertion that variation in a quiddity and its essential constituents is impossible. Their argument is that the factor responsible for variation is present in the stronger instance and absent from the weaker. If it is not included in the quiddity, there is no variation in the quiddity itself. If it is included, the weaker instance does not share the quiddity of the stronger, because some of that quiddity’s constituents are absent from it.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I206.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I207.lower.02","volume":1,"page":207,"physical_pdf_page":220,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"واعترض بأنه جار في العارض أيضا لأن القدر الزائد إما داخل في مفهوم العارض وماهيته فلا اشتراك أو غير داخل فلا تفاوت قال المصنف ما حاصله إن الحق هو أن عدم دخول القدر الزائد الذي به التفاوت في المعنى المشترك إن كان مانعا من التفاوت لزم امتناع التفاوت في شيء من المفهومات في أفراده سواء كان عارضا أو ذاتيا وإن لم يكن مانعا لم يتم ما ذكر في امتناع تفاوت ماهية وذاتياتها انتهى والحاصل أنه لا فرق بين الذاتي والعرضي في امتناع التفاوت بين أفراده وإمكانه","english":"The objection is that the same argument applies to what is incidental. The additional degree either enters into the concept and quiddity of the incidental feature, in which case there is no shared nature, or does not enter into it, in which case there is no variation. The author’s response may be summarised as follows: if the non-inclusion of the additional degree responsible for variation in the shared meaning prevents variation, variation among the instances of any concept must be impossible, whether that concept is incidental or essential. If it does not prevent variation, the proposed argument against variation in a quiddity and its essential constituents fails. End of quotation. The upshot is that essential and incidental predicates do not differ in whether variation among their instances is possible or impossible.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I207.lower.03","volume":1,"page":207,"physical_pdf_page":220,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وأقول الحق أن عدم دخول القدر الزائد إنما يكون مانعا من التفاوت لو لم يكن من جنس المزيد عليه أما لو كان من جنسه فلا شك أن ما فيه ذلك الزائد أشد مما لم يكن فيه ذلك فقد تفاوتا مع اشتراكهما في نفس الماهية سواء كانت تلك الماهية ذاتية أو عرضية","english":"I say: the truth is that non-inclusion of the additional degree prevents variation only if that degree is not of the same kind as what it augments. If it is of the same kind, the thing possessing the addition is undoubtedly stronger than the thing lacking it. The two consequently differ in degree while sharing the very same quiddity, whether that quiddity is essential or incidental.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I207.lower.04","volume":1,"page":207,"physical_pdf_page":220,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إن قيل عدم دخول القدر الزائد على أي معنى من المعاني في ماهيته ينافي كونه من جنسه قلت عدم دخول الشيء في الشيء كما يكون بالخروج عنه يكون بكونه نفسه كما يستدل هو عليه كالمقدار الذي هو ذراع ونصف أشد في المقدارية مما هو ذراع واحد أو نصف والمقدار الزائد أعني نصف الذراع أو الذراع كما أنه ليس جزء المقدار وداخلا فيه كذلك ليس خارجا منه وغير محمول هو عليه إذ المقدار كما يصدق على كل من ذراع ونصف ذراع يصدق على المجموع أيضا لكن صدقه على المجموع أشد من صدقه على كل منهما وهكذا كل","english":"If it is objected that an additional degree’s not entering the quiddity of a meaning is incompatible with its belonging to the same kind, I reply: a thing’s not entering another may result not only from its being outside that other, but also from its being that very thing. This may be illustrated by a magnitude of one and a half cubits: it is greater in magnitude than one cubit or half a cubit. The additional magnitude—the half-cubit or cubit—is not a constituent entering into [the concept of] magnitude, but neither is it external to magnitude or something of which magnitude cannot be predicated. Just as magnitude is true of each of a cubit and half a cubit, it is true of the aggregate; yet it is true of the aggregate to a greater degree than of either part. The same holds of every—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I208.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"كما يستدل هو عليه","reading":"كما يستدل هو عليه","status":"Short illustrative connective is provisionally read; the example and ensuing inference are explicit."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I207.upper.01","volume":1,"page":207,"physical_pdf_page":220,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وكضوء الشمس والسراج وغير ذلك (إلا أن لكل نوع من أنواع اللون اسما خاصا بخلاف الضوء) فإنه إنما يتعين نوعه بالإضافة","english":"[And among lights,] sunlight, lamplight and others. “But each species of colour has a particular name, unlike light,” whose species is specified only by a relation [such as ‘of the sun’ or ‘of the lamp’].","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I206.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I207.upper.02","volume":1,"page":207,"physical_pdf_page":220,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ثم إنه زعم البعض أن لا حقيقة للون أصلا والبياض إنما يتخيل من مخالطة الهواء المضيء للأجسام","english":"Some have claimed that colour has no reality at all: whiteness is merely imagined as a result of luminous air mingling with bodies—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I208.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I208.lower.01","volume":1,"page":208,"physical_pdf_page":221,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ماهية من الماهيات ذاتية أو عرضية جوهرا أو عرضا فتدبر جدا فإنه من معارك آراء الأذكياء","english":"—quiddity, whether essential or incidental, substance or accident. Reflect on this very carefully, for it is a battleground of the opinions of discerning thinkers.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I207.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I208.lower.02","volume":1,"page":208,"physical_pdf_page":221,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله (١) من ذلك الخ) أي من مخالطة الهواء","english":"On “From that, etc.”: that is, from the admixture of air.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The gloss carries a printed marker (1); its lemma refers back to the preceding discussion.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I208.lower.03","volume":1,"page":208,"physical_pdf_page":221,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كونه أمرا حقيقيا الخ) فإنهم ذكروا أن الشيخ الرئيس لم ينكر حصول البياض في نحو الثلج وزبد الماء وأنه لا سبب في ظهوره سوى مخالطة الهواء بل ادعى أنه يحصل بأسباب أخر كما أن الجص يبيض بالطبخ بالنار دون السحق مع أن تفرق الأجزاء وتداخل الهواء فيه في صورة السحق أظهر نعم لابد في أن يكون ظهور البياض على الأبصار بالمخالطة بحيث يظن أنه لا يحصل إلا بها","english":"On “Its being something real, etc.” They report that the Chief Master did not deny the occurrence of whiteness in such things as snow and water-froth, nor that its appearance there has no cause other than the admixture of air. Rather, he maintained that whiteness also arises through other causes: plaster, for example, becomes white through firing, not merely through pulverisation, although the separation of parts and penetration of air are more evident when it is pulverised. Nevertheless, [the point concerns] whiteness’s appearance to sight through admixture, in a way that leads one to suppose that it occurs only through admixture.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"نعم لابد في أن يكون ظهور البياض","reading":"نعم لابد في أن يكون ظهور البياض","status":"The transitional construction is compressed; the English brackets its contextual connection rather than inventing missing Arabic."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I208.lower.04","volume":1,"page":208,"physical_pdf_page":221,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإنه لا بياض حقيقة فيها الخ) الظاهر إسقاط لفظة هنا والحقيقة والمحمول بدل الظهور كما في بعض النسخ كما سبق آنفا من أن ظهور البياض مطلقا أي الإحساس به لا يكون إلا بالمخالطة وإن كان حصوله بسبب آخر غيرها فتفطن","english":"On “There is no real whiteness in them, etc.” The apparent reading is to omit the word “here” [or the indicated qualification] and [to read] “occurrence” in place of “appearance,” as in some copies. As just explained, whiteness’s appearance without restriction—that is, its being sensed—occurs only through admixture, even when its production has another cause. Attend carefully.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"إسقاط لفظة هنا والحقيقة والمحمول بدل الظهور","reading":"إسقاط لفظة هنا والحقيقة والمحمول بدل الظهور","status":"This is a textual-correction note with several difficult words. The proposed changes are not securely reconstructed; retain the source image and mark the whole editorial instruction unresolved."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I208.lower.05","volume":1,"page":208,"physical_pdf_page":221,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله غني عن البيان الخ) فالتعريف بأنه كيفية لا يتوقف الإبصار بها على الإبصار بشيء آخر تنبيه على بعض خواصه (قوله ولغاية الوضوح) ليس في نسخة الشارح التي بيدنا","english":"On “Needs no explanation, etc.” Defining light as a quality whose being seen does not depend on seeing something else merely points to one of its characteristic properties. On “Because of its utmost clarity”: this expression is not in the copy of the commentator’s text in our possession.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I208.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":208,"physical_pdf_page":221,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لا سبب لحصوله) على أنه يمكن أن يقال إنها أيضا من أسباب حصوله ولا دليل يدل على امتناع ذلك اه منه","english":"On “Not a cause of its arising.” Moreover, one may say that admixture is also among the causes of its arising: no proof establishes that this is impossible. End; “from him.”","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The printed signature منه is preserved but does not identify an individual author.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I208.upper.01","volume":1,"page":208,"physical_pdf_page":221,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الشفافة الصغيرة والسواد ضد ذلك أي عدم نفوذ الهواء والضوء في عمق الجسم وباقي الألوان تحصل بحسب اختلاف السبب وتفاوت مخالطة الهواء وأما المحققون فعلى أنها كيفيات حقيقية (وتخيل البياض من مخالطة الضوء للأجسام الشفافة) أي كون البياض في بعض الأجسام أمرا تخيليا لا حقيقة له (كما في الثلج وزبد الماء ومسحوق الزجاج) فإنه لا بياض حقيقة فيها وإنما يتخيل من مخالطة الضوء للأجزاء الصغار الشفافة لتلك الأجسام (لا يمنع هذا التخيل كونه حقيقة) أي كون البياض أمرا حقيقيا موجودا (تحصل بأسباب أخرى غير المخالطة فإنها كما سبب لتخيله لا سبب لحصوله) كما في غير تلك الأجسام مما هو أبيض حقيقة","english":"[The claim concerns] small transparent bodies; blackness is the opposite, namely the failure of air and light to penetrate the body’s depth. Other colours arise in accordance with differences in the cause and in the degree of admixture of air. The investigators, by contrast, hold that colours are real qualities. “Whiteness’s being imagined through the admixture of light with transparent bodies”—that is, whiteness in some bodies being merely imaginary and without a reality—“as in snow, the froth of water and powdered glass,” in which, on this account, no real whiteness exists but is imagined through light mingling with those bodies’ small transparent parts, “does not prevent its being real.” That is, such an imagined appearance does not prevent whiteness from being a real, existent feature “arising through causes other than admixture; for admixture is a cause of its being imagined, not a cause of its arising”—as in other bodies that are genuinely white.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I207.upper.02","readings":[{"locus":"فإنها كما سبب لتخيله","reading":"فإنها كما سبب لتخيله","status":"The printed connective is compressed; the contrast between imagined appearance and real production is explicit."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I208.upper.02","volume":1,"page":208,"physical_pdf_page":221,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والضوء) غني عن البيان كسائر المحسوسات","english":"“Light” needs no explanation, like the other objects of sense.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I209.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.lower.01","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(جعل الشمس ضياء) أي ذات ضياء (والقمر نورا) أي ذا نور","english":"“Made the sun a radiance”: possessing radiance; “and the moon a light”: possessing light.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.lower.02","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله\nوهلم الخ) أي على اختلاف الوسائط بينه وبين المضيء بالذات إلى أن ينتهي\nالضوء ويعدم بالكلية وهو أي عدم الضوء عما من شأنه الضوء هو الظلمة","english":"On “And so on, etc.” According to differences in the intermediaries between it and what is intrinsically luminous, until light comes to an end and disappears entirely. The absence of light from what is capable of it is darkness.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.lower.03","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله\nوإنما المنافي للمجعولية الخ) قد سبق بيان معنى جعل الأعدام فتذكر","english":"On “What is incompatible with being made, etc.” The meaning of making nonexistences was explained earlier; recall it.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.lower.04","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله\nوالظاهر أنث الخ) لاستنادهما إلى ضمير المؤنث أعني الظلمة وإنما قال والظاهر\nلوقوع الفصل أو لاحتمال التأويل بأنه كونهما","english":"On “Apparently they should be feminine, etc.” This is because they are predicated of the pronoun referring to the feminine noun, darkness. He says “apparently” because of the intervening separation, or because an interpretation permitting their wording is possible.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"بأنه كونهما","reading":"بأنه كونهما","status":"The final compressed grammatical explanation is uncertain; the English does not invent its precise supplied noun."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.lower.05","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي كما أنه مانع للخارج الخ) أقول فيه نظر فإن المستدل به\nإما أن يكون ممن يخص المنع بالأمر الوجودي كما هو المقصود فقد اعترف بكون الظلمة وجودية\nفينتقض ما هو بصدده من إثبات كونها عدمية وإما أن يكون ممن يجعل المانع أعم من أن يكون\nوجوديا أو عدميا فيرد أنه لا","english":"On “Just as it prevents someone outside, etc.” I say: there is a difficulty here. Either the argument’s proponent restricts obstruction to something positively existent, as intended, in which case he has admitted that darkness is positively existent, undermining his purpose of establishing that it is privative; or he takes an impediment to include something existent or privative, in which case the objection is that it is not—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I210.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(١٤ - تقريب أول)","english":"Printer’s gathering signature: “14 — Taqrīb, first [volume].”","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.sidenote.01","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"sidenote","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"([قوله؟] المحقق الوقوع مع المعلول) انظر أين الفصل","english":"[On the statement,] “verified as occurring with the effect”: see where the distinguishing clause is.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"rotated outer-margin note","reading":"([قوله؟] المحقق الوقوع مع المعلول) انظر أين الفصل","status":"Provisional reading of a rotated printed sidenote. Exact wording and relation to the running text remain unresolved; inspect the included rotated crop."}],"editorial":"This is a separate outer-margin note. Its uncertain wording is not counted as a resolved translation or merged into the lower commentary.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.upper.01","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وهو (إن كان لذات المحل) بأن لا يكون فائضا عليه من مقابلة جسم آخر مضيء\n(فذاتي كالشمس ويسمى ضياء وإلا فعرضي كالقمر ويسمى نورا) أخذا\nمن قوله تعالى وهو الذي جعل الشمس ضياء والقمر نورا","english":"“If” light “belongs to the subject in virtue of the subject itself”—rather than being bestowed upon it through facing another luminous body—“it is intrinsic, as in the sun, and is called radiance. Otherwise it is extrinsic, as in the moon, and is called light.” The terminology is taken from God’s saying, “He made the sun a radiance and the moon a light.”","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I208.upper.final","editorial":"The quoted terminology is from Q 10:5. “Intrinsic” and “extrinsic” distinguish the source’s modes of illumination, not independent divine causation.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.upper.02","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والعرضي إن كان\nحصوله من مقابلة المضيء لذاته) كضوء القمر وضوء وجه الأرض المقابل\nللشمس (فهو ضوء أول وإلا) بأن كان من مقابلة المضيء لغيره (فضوء\nثان أو ثالث) وهلم","english":"“Extrinsic light, if acquired through facing what is luminous in itself”—like moonlight and the light on the earth’s surface facing the sun—“is primary light. Otherwise”—when acquired through facing something luminous through another—“it is secondary or tertiary light,” and so on.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.upper.03","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والظلمة عدم ملكة له) لا كيفية وجودية على\nما ذهب إليه بعض (ومجعوليتها) كما في قوله تعالى وجعل الظلمات والنور\n(لا توجب كونها كيفية موجودة) فإن الجاعل كما يجعل الوجود يجعل العدم\nالخاص وإنما المنافي للمجعولية العدم الصرف","english":"“Darkness is the privation of light,” not a positively existent quality, as some hold. “Its being made”—as in God’s saying, “He made the darknesses and the light”—“does not require it to be an existent quality.” The maker makes a specific nonexistence just as He makes existence. What is incompatible with being made is sheer nonexistence.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The Qurʾanic phrase is Q 6:1; the distinction between specific and sheer nonexistence belongs to the source argument.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I209.upper.04","volume":1,"page":209,"physical_pdf_page":222,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(كيف ولو كان) أي لو وجد\nوالظاهر أنث الفعلين (لكان حائلا ومانعا للجالس في الغار) المظلم\n(من إبصار الخارج) من الغار (كالعكس) أي كما أنه مانع للخارج من إبصار\nما هو في الغار (لعدم الفارق) لوجود العائق من الرؤية بينهما في الصورتين","english":"“How could it be? If it existed”—the two verbs apparently should be feminine—“it would obstruct the person sitting in a” dark “cave from seeing what is outside, as in the reverse case.” That is, just as it prevents someone outside from seeing what is in the cave, “there being no difference”: the supposed impediment to vision would lie between them in both cases.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The grammatical criticism of the masculine verbs is translated as part of the sharḥ, not silently corrected away.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I210.lower.01","volume":1,"page":210,"physical_pdf_page":223,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"يجوز أن تكون مانعيتها على تقدير وجودها مشروطة بشرط يوجد في بعض الصور\nكما في صورة العكس دون بعض كما في الصورة الأولى فيكون قوله لعدم الفارق\nممنوعا لجواز الفرق بين الصورتين على هذا التقدير كما بين الفرق بينهما على تقدير\nكونها عدمية بقوله وحينئذ اختلاف حالهما الخ فتدبر","english":"[Why is it not] possible that, supposing darkness existent, its obstructing character is conditioned by something present in some cases, as in the reverse case, but not others, as in the first? “There being no difference” is then denied: there may be a difference between the cases on this supposition, just as he explained a difference on the supposition that darkness is privative in “The two cases then differ,” and so forth. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I209.lower.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I210.lower.02","volume":1,"page":210,"physical_pdf_page":223,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وفي المواقف الخ)\nحاصله أنه لم لا يجوز أن تكون الظلمة وجودية لكن مانعيتها من الإبصار تكون\nمشروطة بشرط يوجد في صورة العكس دون الصورة الأولى كما أن الضوء وجودي\nوسببيته للرؤية مشروطة بشرط يوجد في بعض الصور دون بعض فهو راجع إلى\nما أوردناه آنفا فتأمل","english":"On “Al-Mawāqif says, etc.” In summary: why may darkness not be positively existent, but its obstructing vision depend on a condition found in the reverse case and not the first? Light is itself positively existent, yet its causing vision depends on a condition present in some cases and not others. This returns to our preceding objection. Consider this.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I210.lower.03","volume":1,"page":210,"physical_pdf_page":223,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي ما يتلألأ الخ) تفسير للمشرق أي الذاتي من الضوء\nالذي له تلألؤ وتلألؤه حتى كأنه شيء يفيض من المحل ويضطرب مجيئا وذهابا","english":"On “What glitters, etc.” This explains “shining”: intrinsically sourced light that glitters, its glittering being such that something seems to flow from the subject and oscillate to and fro.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I210.lower.04","volume":1,"page":210,"physical_pdf_page":223,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله من التلألؤ واللمعان) بيان لكلمة منه كما في الحاشية لكن فيه أنها عبارة عن الضوء\nالمتلألئ لا عن التلألؤ كما هو ظاهر","english":"On “From glittering and gleaming.” This explains the pronoun in “of it”, as the gloss states. But the referent is the glittering light, not glittering itself, as is evident.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I210.lower.05","volume":1,"page":210,"physical_pdf_page":223,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله دفعة الخ) إن قيل إن الحدوث دفعة\nينافي الحركة فإنها تدريجية لا دفعية فكيف يتوهم كون ذلك حركة قلت إن\nالدفعي هو حدوثه في المستضيء لا انتقاله من المضيء والذي توهموه حركة هو\nالانتقال لا الحدوث فتفطن","english":"On “All at once, etc.” If it is objected that occurrence all at once contradicts motion, which is gradual rather than instantaneous, so how could this be mistaken for motion?—I reply: what occurs all at once is light’s occurrence in the illuminated object, not its passage from the luminous object. What they took to be motion is that passage, not the occurrence. Attend carefully.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I210.lower.06","volume":1,"page":210,"physical_pdf_page":223,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله يخلقه الله تعالى الخ) الأولى ترك هذا القيد فإن","english":"On “Which God creates, etc.” It would be preferable to omit this qualification, for—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I211.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I210.upper.01","volume":1,"page":210,"physical_pdf_page":223,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فتعين أنها عدم الضوء وحينئذ اختلاف حالهما بانتفاء شرط كون الجالس في الغار\nمرئيا فلا يرى بدون شرط كون الخارج مرئيا فيرى وفي المواقف أقول كأن شرط\nالرؤية ضوء يحيط بالمرئي فقد يكون العائق عن الرؤية ظلمة محيطة به لم يكن بعيدا","english":"It is therefore established that darkness is the absence of light. The two cases differ because a condition for the person in the cave’s being visible is absent, so that he is not seen, whereas the condition for the outside person’s being visible is not absent, so that he is seen. Al-Mawāqif says: I say, it would not be far-fetched that the condition of vision is light surrounding the visible thing, so that an impediment to vision may be darkness surrounding it.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I209.upper.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I210.upper.02","volume":1,"page":210,"physical_pdf_page":223,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والذاتي منه المشرق) أي ما يتلألأ ويلمع (كالشمس) من التلألؤ\nواللمعان (يسمى شعاعا والعرضي منه كالمرآة) التي حاذت الشمس\n(يسمى بريقا) ونسبة البريق إلى الشعاع نسبة النور إلى الضياء","english":"“Its shining intrinsic kind”—what glitters and gleams, “as with the sun”—“is called a ray. Its extrinsic kind, as in a mirror” facing the sun, “is called gleam.” Gleam stands to ray as borrowed light stands to radiance.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I210.upper.03","volume":1,"page":210,"physical_pdf_page":223,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وقد يتوهم\nأن الضوء أجسام صغار تنفصل من المضيء وتتصل بالمستضيء) وإنما توهم\nذلك (بناء على أن حدوثه) أي حدوث الضوء في المستضيء دفعة يخلقه الله\nتعالى (من) مقابلة (مضيء عال كالشمس أو) من مضيء (متحرك)","english":"“One may suppose that light consists of small bodies separating from the luminous object and reaching the illuminated one.” This supposition is based on the fact that its occurrence—the occurrence of light in the illuminated object, all at once, which God creates—“from” facing “a luminous object above, such as the sun, or” from a “moving” luminous object—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I211.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I211.lower.01","volume":1,"page":211,"physical_pdf_page":224,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"القائلين بأن الضوء ليس إلا عرضا حادثا في المستضيء بالمقابل من غير حركة ليسوا المتكلمين\nفقط بل هم وكثير من محققي الفلاسفة كما لا يخفى فافهم","english":"[It would be preferable to omit that qualification because] those who hold that light is only an accident occurring in the illuminated object through facing the source, without motion, are not only theologians: many philosophical investigators hold it too. This is evident. Understand.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I210.lower.06","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I211.lower.02","volume":1,"page":211,"physical_pdf_page":224,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والحركة بالذات الخ)\nوإنما قيل بالذات لأن الأعراض تتحرك بتبعية المحل","english":"On “Motion in itself, etc.” “In itself” is specified because accidents move in dependence on their subjects.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I211.lower.03","volume":1,"page":211,"physical_pdf_page":224,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والحركة بالطبع تمتنع\nالخ) أقول هذا وجه من الوجوه التي ذكرها في شرح المقاصد لكن فيه تأمل\nفإن الحركة بالطبع لا تمتنع إلى الجهات المختلفة إلا إذا كانت من البسائط وحدوث\nضوء الشمس لما كان من البسيط ليس إلا بالانحدار من العلو إلى السفل فليس مختلف\nالجهات وأما ضوء نحو السراج فلا نسلم أنه من البسائط فلا مانع من حركته إلى\nالجهات المختلفة","english":"On “Natural motion cannot, etc.” I say: this is one of the arguments given in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, but it requires consideration. Natural motion towards different directions is impossible only for simples. Sunlight, being from a simple, occurs only by descent from above to below; its directions do not differ. As for a lamp’s light, we do not concede that it is from a simple, so nothing prevents motion in different directions.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I211.lower.04","volume":1,"page":211,"physical_pdf_page":224,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"هذا ومن تلك الوجوه أن الضوء لو كان جسما ولا خفاء في أنه\nمحسوس بالبصر لكان ساترا للجسم الذي يحيط به ولو سلم عدم لزوم كونه ساترا فلا\nخفاء أنه حائل في الجملة فيلزم أن يكون الأكثر ضوءا أقل ظهورا وأصعب رؤية\nلأن تكون أمون على إدراك الباصرة وليس كذلك","english":"Another argument is that, if light were a body—and it is plainly visible—it would conceal the body it surrounds. Even if concealment did not necessarily follow, light would at least interpose something. Greater illumination would therefore make a thing less manifest and harder to see, [rather than easier] for visual apprehension. But that is not so.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"لأن تكون أمون","reading":"لا أن تكون أهون","status":"Probable negative comparative clause; confirm enlarged original before finalising Arabic."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I211.lower.05","volume":1,"page":211,"physical_pdf_page":224,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وفي هذا تأمل أيضا لجواز أن\nيكون هذا النوع من الأجسام بحيث يكون توسطه شرطا للرؤية كما أنه ربما يستعان","english":"This too requires consideration: this kind of body might be such that its interposition is a condition of vision, just as one sometimes employs—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I212.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I211.upper.01","volume":1,"page":211,"physical_pdf_page":224,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"كالسراج المنقول من موضع إلى آخر (أو من متوسط بينه) أي المضيء (وبين\nالمستضيء يوهم) هذا الحدوث الدفعي (حركة انحدارا) من الشمس إلى الأرض\n(واتباعا) للسراج في الانتقال (وانعكاسا) من المتوسط إلى المستضيء وكل\nمن ذلك حركة بالذات والحركة بالذات إنما هي للجسم ضرورة","english":"[A moving source,] like a lamp carried from place to place, “or from something intermediate between” the luminous and “the illuminated object, suggests”—through the instantaneous occurrence—“a descending motion” from the sun to earth, “a following motion” accompanying the lamp’s relocation, or “a reflection” from the intermediary towards the illuminated object. Each would be motion in itself, which necessarily belongs only to a body.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I210.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I211.upper.02","volume":1,"page":211,"physical_pdf_page":224,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ووجه كونه وهما\nأنه لو كان ذلك حركة لكانت بالطبع إذ لا قسر ولا إرادة والحركة بالطبع تمتنع\nإلى جهات مختلفة","english":"The reason this is a mistaken supposition is that, were it motion, it would be natural: there is neither constraint nor will. Natural motion, however, cannot be towards different directions.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I211.upper.03","volume":1,"page":211,"physical_pdf_page":224,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وعدم رؤية اللون في الظلمة قيل لكون الضوء شرطا لوجوده)\nفإن اللون تضعف بحسب ضعف الضوء وكلما كان الضوء أقوى كان اللون أشد\nفكل طبقة من الضوء شرط لطبقة من اللون فإذا انتفى طبقات الأضواء كلها انتفى","english":"“The failure to see colour in darkness is said to be because light is a condition of colour’s existence.” Colour weakens as light weakens, and the stronger the light, the more intense the colour. Each degree of light is therefore a condition of a degree of colour; when all degrees of light are absent, all—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I212.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I212.lower.01","volume":1,"page":212,"physical_pdf_page":225,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"بالزجاج والبلور على إبصار الخطوط الدقيقة عند ضعف الباصرة كما هو العادة","english":"[One sometimes employs] glass and crystal to see fine writing when vision is weak, as is customary.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I211.lower.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I212.lower.02","volume":1,"page":212,"physical_pdf_page":225,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله\nوإنكار ذلك قريب من الضروريات) ومنهم من ذهب إلى أن الضوء ليس مغايرا للون\nبل هو عبارة عن ظهور اللون وليس له ولا لمن يدعي كونه شرطا لوجود اللون تمسك\nيعتد به بل ربما يتمسك على بطلانهما أما على الأول فبوجود الضوء بدون اللون كما في\nالبلور بالليل وأما على الثاني فإن قبول الجسم للضوء مشروط بوجود اللون فلو كان\nوجود اللون مشروطا بوجود الضوء لزم الدور فتأمل","english":"On “Denying that approaches [denying] necessary truths.” Some hold that light does not differ from colour, but means colour’s manifestation. Neither they nor those who claim light is a condition of colour’s existence have a worthwhile argument. Indeed, arguments may be made against both. Against the first: light occurs without colour, as with crystal at night. Against the second: a body’s reception of light requires colour; if colour’s existence required light, a circle would result. Consider this.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The example of crystal at night is retained as stated in the historical source, not silently corrected to modern optical terminology.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I212.lower.03","volume":1,"page":212,"physical_pdf_page":225,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ثم إنه قد يتوهم الخ)\nالحاصل أن هناك ثلاث مطالب الأول أن حدوث الصوت عند وصول الهواء إلى الحس\nليس في الحس فقط كما توهمه بعض بل هو موجود خارج الحس أيضا الثاني ليس\nالمسموع هو الصوت القائم بالهواء الواصل فقط بل به وبالهواء الخارج أيضا ويدل\nعليهما إدراك جهة الصوت والتميز بين قريبه وبعيده الثالث أن إدراك الصوت","english":"On “One may then suppose, etc.” In summary, three claims are involved. First, when sound occurs as air reaches the sense faculty, it is not only in the faculty, as some suppose; it also exists outside it. Second, the audible object is not only the sound inhering in arriving air, but in that air and external air together. Apprehension of sound’s direction and discrimination of near and far support both. Third, apprehension of sound—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I213.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I212.upper.01","volume":1,"page":212,"physical_pdf_page":225,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"طبقات الألوان بأسرها (والحق أنه شرط لرؤيته) وإنكار ذلك قريب من إنكار\nالضروريات","english":"[When all degrees of light are absent,] all degrees of colour would be absent. “The truth is that light is a condition for seeing colour.” Denying that approaches denying necessary truths.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I211.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I212.upper.02","volume":1,"page":212,"physical_pdf_page":225,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وأما المسموعات) التي هي القسم الثالث من الكيفيات\nالمحسوسة (فالأصوات) والصوت عندنا يحدث بمحض خلق الله تعالى\n(وعند الفلاسفة سببه القريب تموج الهواء المعلول) هذا التموج (للقرع)\nأي الإمساك الشديد (أو القلع) أي التفريق الشديد وهما سببان معدان\nله","english":"“Audible qualities,” the third division of sensible qualities, “are sounds.” On our account, sound occurs solely through God’s creation. “For the philosophers its proximate cause is the undulation of air caused by impact”—forceful pressing together—“or separation”—forceful pulling apart. These are two preparatory causes for it.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I212.upper.03","volume":1,"page":212,"physical_pdf_page":225,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ثم إنه قد يتوهم أنه لا وجود للصوت في الخارج عن الصماخ وإنما يحدث في\nالحس عند وصول الهواء المتموج إلى الصماخ ولهم تردد في أنه إذا وصل الهواء\nإلى الصماخ فالمسموع هو الصوت القائم بالهواء الواصل فقط أو به وبالهواء الخارج\nأيضا","english":"One may then suppose that sound has no existence outside the auditory passage and occurs only in the sense faculty when undulating air reaches that passage. There is also uncertainty whether, when air reaches it, the audible object is only the sound inhering in the arriving air, or sound inhering both in that air and in the external air.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I212.upper.04","volume":1,"page":212,"physical_pdf_page":225,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ويدل على وجوده خارج الصماخ وعلى تعلق الإحساس به هنالك) أي\nخارج الصماخ وأنه ليس المسموع مجرد الصوت القائم بالهواء الواصل أمران\nأحدهما (إدراك جهته ولو من الجانب المخالف) للأذن السامعة ولو لم يوجد\nإلا في الحس ولم يكن المسموع إلا الصوت القائم بالهواء الواصل لما أدركنا عند","english":"Two things “indicate that sound exists outside the auditory passage and that sensation relates to it there”—outside the passage—so that the audible object is not merely sound inhering in arriving air. First, “apprehending its direction, even from the opposite side” to the listening ear. If it existed only in the sense faculty, and the audible object were only sound inhering in arriving air, then on—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I213.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I213.lower.01","volume":1,"page":213,"physical_pdf_page":226,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"موقوف على وصول الهواء إلى الحس ويدل عليه ميله مع الرياح والتفرد بسماعه","english":"[Apprehension is] dependent on air reaching the sense faculty. Its inclination with the wind and the exclusivity of hearing it indicate this.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I212.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I213.lower.02","volume":1,"page":213,"physical_pdf_page":226,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله\nنعرف جهته الخ) إن قيل يجوز أن يكون إدراك الجهة لأجل أن الهواء المتموج\nيجيء من تلك الجهة لا لأن الصوت موجود خارج الحس فيها والإحساس متعلق\nبه هناك أيضا وأجيب بأنه لو كان كذلك لم يدرك جهة الصوت بل كان المدرك هو\nجهة الهواء الجائي فقط مع أن جهة الصوت مدركة أيضا كما لا يخفى","english":"On “We recognise its direction, etc.” If it is said that direction might be apprehended because the undulating air comes from that direction, not because sound exists outside the sense faculty in that direction and sensation relates to it there as well—the reply is that, were this so, the direction of sound would not be apprehended, only that of the arriving air. Yet the direction of sound is also apprehended, as is evident.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I213.lower.03","volume":1,"page":213,"physical_pdf_page":226,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لم يحصل\nالتميز المذكور الخ) إن قيل يجوز أن يكون تميز القريب من البعيد لأجل أن الأثر القارع\nالقريب أقوى من البعيد وإن لم يكن الصوت موجودا في الجهة والمسافة ولم يتعلق\nالإحساس به هناك أجيب بأنه لو صح ذلك لزم أن يشتبه الضعيف القريب بالقوي\nالبعيد مع أن التميز حاصل بينهما","english":"On “This distinction could not occur, etc.” If it is said that near and far may be distinguished because the impact from a near source is stronger than that from a distant one, even without sound existing along that direction and distance or sensation relating to it there—the reply is that this would make a weak nearby sound indistinguishable from a strong distant one, whereas they are distinguished.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I213.lower.04","volume":1,"page":213,"physical_pdf_page":226,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إن قيل مدرك السمع ليس إلا الصوت فلا معنى\nلإدراك جهة ضرورة أنها ليست من المسموعات أجيب بأن معنى إدراك الجهة\nههنا بسماع الصوت في الجهة نعرف أن ذلك الصوت من تلك الجهة لا أن المدرك لها\nهو السمع وذلك كما نعرف بذوق الحلاوة من هذا الجسم أنها منه وإن لم يكن الجسم من\nالمذوقات","english":"If it is said that hearing apprehends only sound, so that apprehending a direction has no meaning—direction necessarily not being an audible object—the reply is that “apprehending direction” here means that, by hearing a sound in a direction, we know it is from that direction, not that hearing itself apprehends the direction. Likewise, by tasting sweetness from a body we know that it comes from that body, although the body is not itself one of the tastes.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I213.lower.05","volume":1,"page":213,"physical_pdf_page":226,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لعدم وصول الهواء إلى صماخه الخ) قد يقال لو كان السماع\nمتوقفا على وصول الهواء لما أدركنا جهة الصوت ولا حده من القرب والبعد لأن\nالواصل هو الذي في الصماخ والجواب ما سبق آنفا من أن الصوت الموقوف إدراكه","english":"On “Because air does not reach his auditory passage, etc.” It may be said that, if hearing depended on air’s arrival, we would apprehend neither the sound’s direction nor its degree of nearness or distance, since what arrives is what is in the auditory passage. The reply is the point just made: the sound whose apprehension depends—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I214.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I213.upper.01","volume":1,"page":213,"physical_pdf_page":226,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"سماعه جهة مخالفة للسامعة واللازم باطل لأنا إذا سمعنا صوتا نعرف جهته ولو\nمن الجانب المخالف (و) الأمر الثاني هو (التميز بين قريبه وبعيده) فلو لم\nيوجد خارج الصماخ ولم يتعلق الإحساس به هنالك لم يحصل التميز المذكور واللازم\nباطل","english":"[It would follow that, on] hearing it, [we could not discern] a direction different from the hearing faculty’s own. The consequent is false: when we hear a sound we recognise its direction, even from the opposite side. The second consideration is “distinguishing its nearness and distance.” If it did not exist outside the auditory passage, with sensation relating to it there, this distinction could not occur; but that consequence is false.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I212.upper.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I213.upper.02","volume":1,"page":213,"physical_pdf_page":226,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) يدل (على كون إدراكه بوصول الهواء أشديته مع الرياح) ولا يسمعه\nمن كان مهب الرياح من جهته لعدم وصول الهواء إلى صماخه (وأنه ينفرد\nبسماعه من ينفرد بذلك) كمن وضع طرف أنبوبة في فمه وطرفها الآخر في\nصماخ إنسان وتكلم فيها سمعه ذلك الإنسان دون غيره من الحاضرين","english":"“Its greater intensity with the wind” indicates that “its apprehension occurs through the arrival of air.” Someone from whose direction the wind blows does not hear it, because the air does not reach his auditory passage. Another indication is that “hearing it is confined to the one to whom that arrival is confined.” If someone places one end of a tube in his mouth and the other in another person’s auditory passage, then speaks into it, that person hears him but the others present do not.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The acoustical explanation is the historical argument being translated, not a modern physical claim.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I214.lower.01","volume":1,"page":214,"physical_pdf_page":227,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"على وصول الهواء ليس هو القائم بالواصل فقط بل به وبالخارج أيضا","english":"[The sound whose apprehension depends] on the air’s arrival does not inhere only in what arrives, but also in what remains outside.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I213.lower.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I214.lower.02","volume":1,"page":214,"physical_pdf_page":227,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وما ذلك\nإلا لمنع الأنبوبة الخ) قد يقال إن غاية ما ذكر هو الدوران وهو لا يفيد القطع\nبالسببية وأجيب بأن تلك الأمارة من الأمارات التي ربما تفيد اليقين الحدسي للناظر\nوإن لم تقم حجة على المناظر","english":"On “This is only because the tube prevents, etc.” It may be said that at most this establishes concomitance, not certainty about causation. The reply is that this is one of the indications that may give an investigator intuitive certainty, although it does not establish a proof against a disputant.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I214.lower.03","volume":1,"page":214,"physical_pdf_page":227,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فهو الصدى الخ) في شرح المقاصد أنهم ترددوا في\nحدوثه هل هو من تموج الهواء الأول الراجع على هيئته أو من تموج هواء آخر\nبيننا وبين المقاوم تكيف بكيفية الهواء الراجع وهذا هو الأشبه","english":"On “It is the echo, etc.” Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid reports uncertainty over its production: does it arise from the undulation of the original air returning in its configuration, or from another body of air between us and the resisting object, qualified by the configuration of the returning air? The latter is more plausible.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I214.lower.04","volume":1,"page":214,"physical_pdf_page":227,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله احترازا الخ)\nأي التقييد بالمماثلة فيما ذكر للاحتراز الخ","english":"On “This excludes, etc.” It is the restriction of equality in the stated respect that excludes [high and low pitch themselves].","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I214.lower.05","volume":1,"page":214,"physical_pdf_page":227,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فيمتاز بهما ذلك الصوت الخ) أي\nتميزا في المسموع","english":"On “By which that sound differs, etc.” That is, a distinction in what is heard.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false}]);